EXAM_DATA.papers["2025-english-one"] = {"id":"2025-english-one","year":2025,"label":"2025 英语一","era":"modern","sourceUrl":"https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/kaoyan/paper/2025-english-one/","timeLimitMin":180,"scoreMode":"estimated","parts":[{"id":"section1","kind":"cloze","title":"Section I 完形填空","directions":"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section1-1","text":"Located in the southern Peloponnesian peninsula, Pavlopetri (the modern name of the site) emerged as a Neolithic settlement around 3500 B.C. and became an important trading center for Mycenaean Greece (1650–1180 B.C.). This area of the Aegean Sea is   {{1}}   to earthquakes and tsunamis, which caused the city to   {{2}}   sink. The slow sea level rise in the Mediterranean   {{3}}   the city more than 3,000 years ago."},{"id":"p-section1-2","text":"For millennia, the city's   {{4}}   lay unseen below some 13 feet of water. They were covered by a thick layer of sand   {{5}}   the island of Laconia. In recent decades, shifting   {{6}}   and climate change have eroded a natural barrier that   {{7}}   Pavlopetri. In 1967 a scientific survey of the Peloponnesian coast was   {{8}}   data to analyze changes in sea levels   {{9}}   British oceanographer Nicholas Flemming first spotted the sunken   {{10}}   . A year later, he returned with a few students to   {{11}}   the location and map the site. The team identified some 15 buildings, courtyards, a network of streets, and two chamber tombs.   {{12}}   the exciting initial finds, the site would lie   {{13}}   for decades before archaeologists would return."},{"id":"p-section1-3","text":"In 2009 archaeologists Chrysanthi Gallou and Jon Henderson   {{14}}   the excavation of Pavlopetri in cooperation with the Greek Ministry of Culture. Since the 1960s, underwater archaeology   {{15}}   and tools had made huge advances. The team   {{16}}   robotics, sonar mapping, and state-of-the-art graphics to survey the site. From 2009 to 2013 they were able to bring the underwater town to   {{17}}   . Covering about two and a half acres, Pavlopetri's three main roads   {{18}}   some 50 rectangular buildings, all of which had open courtyards. Excavations revealed a large number of Minoan-style loom weights,   {{19}}   Pavlopetri was a thriving trade center with a   {{20}}   textile industry."}]},"questions":[{"n":1,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"relevant"},{"key":"B","text":"prone"},{"key":"C","text":"available"},{"key":"D","text":"alien"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"1.","reason":"to earthquakes and tsunamis 锁定\"易遭受\"语义 线索就在本句内：is __(1)__ to earthquakes and tsunamis, which caused the city to __(2)__ sink——后半句说地震与海啸\"导致城市下沉\"，反推空处必须表达\"易遭受地震海啸\"，候选词须能与 to 搭配且承载\"易受……影响\"之义。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"be relevant to 意为\"与……相关\"，是中性的\"相关性\"，无法表达\"易遭受灾害\"这一受害方向；放回is __(1)__ to earthquakes and tsunamis只会变成\"与地震海啸有关\"，与下半句\"导致城市下沉\"的因果不衔接。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"be prone to + n. 是英语里的固定表达，意为\"易于、有……倾向\"，常用于易发自然灾害；代入后\"该海域易遭受地震和海啸\"，与下文caused the city to __(2)__ sink的因果链严丝合缝。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"available to 一般搭配人或机构（对某人开放/可获得），与\"地震海啸\"这种自然灾害事件搭配不通；说\"海域对地震海啸是可获得的\"在中文里就立即露馅。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"be alien to 意为\"与……格格不入、对……陌生\"，恰恰是\"不易遭受\"的反向；与下半句因地震海啸而下沉的事实矛盾。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":2,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"accidentally"},{"key":"B","text":"frequently"},{"key":"C","text":"gradually"},{"key":"D","text":"temporarily"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"2.","reason":"下句 slow sea level rise 直接呼应 跨句线索就在下一句：The slow sea level rise in the Mediterranean __(3)__ the city more than 3,000 years ago——\"地中海缓慢的海平面上升\"明确把沉没过程定性为长期、渐进，本空必须与\"slow\"同向。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"accidentally 强调\"偶然\"，但城市下沉是地质过程，不是偶发事件，与下句slow sea level rise的渐进性也不合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"frequently 表\"频繁\"，意味着多次发生，但城市\"下沉\"是一个一次性的长期演变，并非反复；与 slow 也不同向。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"gradually \"逐渐地\"正是 slow sea level rise 的同义改写，把\"下沉\"刻画为长期渐进过程，与全段地质成因的语境完全吻合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"temporarily 表\"暂时地\"，而城市此后lay unseen below some 13 feet of water数千年，完全不是暂时性下沉；逻辑反向。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":3,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"disguised"},{"key":"B","text":"submerged"},{"key":"C","text":"relocated"},{"key":"D","text":"isolated"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"3.","reason":"上句已说城市下沉、下段又说在水下 线索跨句：上一句caused the city to __(2)__ sink+下一段the city's __(4)__ lay unseen below some 13 feet of water共同把城市的最终状态钉死为\"沉没于水下\"，本空必须表达\"海水使城市被淹没\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"disguise 意为\"伪装、掩饰\"，主语通常是有意图的施动者或具有遮盖功能的物，且与城市下沉的物理过程不搭。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"submerge \"使没入水中\"恰好对应海平面上升将城市\"淹没\"的物理过程，与下一段lay unseen below some 13 feet of water的结果状态完全衔接。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"relocate 表\"重新安置、迁移\"，城市的位置并未改变，是水位上升把城市淹了；语义错位。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"isolate \"使孤立\"侧重切断联系，与海水彻底覆盖城市的物理事实不匹配。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":4,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"legends"},{"key":"B","text":"programs"},{"key":"C","text":"remains"},{"key":"D","text":"surroundings"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"4.","reason":"下文清点建筑、街道、墓室 跨段呼应：The team identified some 15 buildings, courtyards, a network of streets, and two chamber tombs与Pavlopetri's three main roads __(18)__ some 50 rectangular buildings说明水下保留的是建筑与街道，本空必须表\"遗迹/遗存\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"legends 是\"传说\"，属语言/叙述层面，不能物理上\"lay below water\"；与下文清点的实物（建筑、街道）也对不上。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"programs \"项目/计划/节目\"是抽象事务，不能沉在海底；与本段历史遗存语境无关。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"remains 表\"遗迹、遗存\"，正好涵盖15 buildings, courtyards, a network of streets, and two chamber tombs这些被发掘的实物，能与\"lay unseen below water\"自然搭配。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"surroundings 指\"周围环境\"，强调外围而非城市本体，但本段要谈的是城市自身被埋的部分（建筑、街道、墓室），不是周边环境。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":5,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"across"},{"key":"B","text":"off"},{"key":"C","text":"under"},{"key":"D","text":"via"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"5.","reason":"上一段已交代位于希腊本土近海 线索来自第一段开头Located in the southern Peloponnesian peninsula, Pavlopetri——遗址位于伯罗奔尼撒半岛南端近海，沙层位于\"拉科尼亚岛沿岸/近海\"，方位关系是\"离岸不远\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"across 表\"横跨\"，意味着穿越岛屿或从一端到另一端，与\"沙层位于岛屿附近海域\"的实际方位不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"off the island of Laconia 是地理表达定式，意为\"在拉科尼亚岛沿岸近海\"，与全文\"水下古城\"的语境完美匹配。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"under 意为\"在……下方\"，但沙层不可能位于整座岛屿下方；岛屿是陆地，与沙层是近海覆盖物的关系不同。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"via 表\"经由\"，是路径介词，与静态位置描述无关。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":6,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"currents"},{"key":"B","text":"rivers"},{"key":"C","text":"seasons"},{"key":"D","text":"winds"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"6.","reason":"海洋环境下\"shifting + 侵蚀屏障\"指洋流 线索就在本句：shifting __(6)__ and climate change have eroded a natural barrier that __(7)__ Pavlopetri——海洋环境里能\"shifting\"并\"侵蚀沿海屏障\"的自然要素，是海流；季节/风/河流要么与海洋侵蚀不直接、要么搭 shifting 不自然。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"currents 既常与 shifting 搭配描述方向改变的洋流，又是侵蚀海岸屏障的直接动力，与下句eroded a natural barrier that __(7)__ Pavlopetri衔接最紧。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"rivers 是河流，主要在陆地与河口起作用，而本句的屏障位于海洋一侧（保护已沉于地中海的遗址），用河流来\"shifting\"侵蚀这里的屏障不自然。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"shifting seasons 虽可指\"季节更迭\"，但季节本身不是直接侵蚀海岸屏障的物理力量，与 climate change 也几近重复。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"shifting winds 虽然可说，但风对水下沙层屏障的直接侵蚀作用不及洋流明显，且海洋语境里 currents 更对位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":7,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"elevated"},{"key":"B","text":"separated"},{"key":"C","text":"comprised"},{"key":"D","text":"protected"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"7.","reason":"前句\"eroded barrier\"暗含失去保护 线索就在主句：eroded a natural barrier that __(7)__ Pavlopetri——屏障被侵蚀，导致古城此后失去保护而暴露，反推屏障原本承担的是\"保护\"功能。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"elevate 表\"抬高、提升\"，屏障并未把古城\"抬高\"，与下沉于水下的事实矛盾。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"separate 强调\"使分离\"，但本句要表达的是屏障\"保护\"古城免遭侵蚀，而非把古城与外界隔开；语义偏离主旨。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"comprise 意为\"包含/构成\"，屏障并未\"包含\"整座古城；逻辑关系不对。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"protect 正好与 barrier 的本职功能对位，表\"保护\"，与\"屏障被侵蚀导致古城重见水下\"的因果链严密匹配。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":8,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"gathering"},{"key":"B","text":"restoring"},{"key":"C","text":"updating"},{"key":"D","text":"supplying"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"语法形态","stemZh":"8.","reason":"to analyze changes 暗示\"为分析而收集\" 线索在不定式：was __(8)__ data to analyze changes in sea levels——目的是分析海平面变化，这要求先\"收集\"数据；逻辑顺序是\"收集→分析\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"gather data 是高频搭配，与不定式 to analyze 形成\"先收集后分析\"的标准科研流程，主语 survey 主动收集数据，时态语态均合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"restore data 意为\"恢复/还原数据\"，常指数据丢失后的恢复，与首次科考的\"采集\"语境不符。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"update data 需要已有数据基础，可这次科考刚开始，文中并未提到既有数据集，无从\"更新\"。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"supply data 表\"向外提供数据\"，主语成了供给方，但 survey 本身是采集方，与 to analyze 的目的不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":9,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"when"},{"key":"B","text":"until"},{"key":"C","text":"after"},{"key":"D","text":"once"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"9.","reason":"首次发现发生在科考进行中 线索是动作的时间叠合：was __(8)__ data是一段进行中的科考；首次发现古城正发生在这段过程中，本空需要表达\"正当……时\"或\"就在那时\"的同时关系。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"when 引导\"正当此时\"，与主句\"调查正在采集数据\"配合，表达\"科考进行中弗莱明偶然发现沉没结构\"的同时关系，文意流畅。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"until 表\"直到……才/才停止\"，会暗示调查为发现古城而进行，但调查目的是分析海平面，发现古城只是意外；语义偏。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"after 把发现古城放在调查结束之后，但文中显示发现发生在调查进行中，而非之后。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"once 强调\"一旦……就\"，常用条件触发，但这里只是偶然撞见，并非条件成立后必然发生。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":10,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"belongings"},{"key":"B","text":"resources"},{"key":"C","text":"products"},{"key":"D","text":"structures"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"10.","reason":"下文清点的建筑+街道+墓室对应 structures 线索来自紧邻下句：The team identified some 15 buildings, courtyards, a network of streets, and two chamber tombs——这些都是\"建筑/构筑物\"，对应统称应是\"structures\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"belongings 偏向\"个人随身物品\"，与15座建筑、街道、墓室这种宏观建筑物不在同一范畴。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"resources 指\"资源\"，过于抽象笼统，且不能直接对应建筑物清单。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"products 指\"产品\"，多用于工业制成品；考古中描述沉于海底的建筑物不会用这个词。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"structures 涵盖建筑、街道网、墓室等人工构筑物，与下句清单形成统称—分项的对应，与 sunken 也搭配自然。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":11,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"preserve"},{"key":"B","text":"select"},{"key":"C","text":"display"},{"key":"D","text":"examine"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"11.","reason":"and map the site 提示先勘查再绘图 线索就在并列结构：to __(11)__ the location and map the site——map the site 是\"绘制遗址图\"，其前必有\"实地察看/检查\"的环节，本空对应该阶段。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"preserve 表\"保护、保存\"，是后期文物维护阶段动作，与首次回返实地勘查不符。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"select \"选择\"宾语通常是众多对象中的部分，但这里只有一处遗址，无从\"挑选\"。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"display 表\"展示\"，与现场科考无关；此时遗址刚被发现，还谈不上展示。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"examine the location 即\"实地察看现场\"，与后续\"map the site\"形成自然的工作链：先察看，再制图，符合科考流程。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":12,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Despite"},{"key":"B","text":"Unlike"},{"key":"C","text":"Besides"},{"key":"D","text":"Among"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"逻辑关系","stemZh":"12.","reason":"exciting finds vs. lie undisturbed for decades 前半句强调\"令人兴奋的初步发现\"，后半句却是\"遗址此后数十年无人问津\"——一正一负，明显是让步转折。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"Despite + 名词短语表\"尽管……\"，正好把\"令人兴奋的初步发现\"与\"此后数十年无人问津\"连成让步—主句的对照，文意贴合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"Unlike 引出\"与……不同\"的比较项，但后半句的主语 site 并不与\"initial finds\"做横向比较，逻辑不合。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"Besides 表\"除……之外还\"，是补充并列，但前后是反向，使用 Besides 会让\"令人兴奋的发现\"与\"无人问津\"被并列叙述，明显矛盾。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"Among 表\"在……之中\"，后接复数集合作范围，但此处不需要表\"位于若干发现之中\"，与主句逻辑无关。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":13,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"unchallenged"},{"key":"B","text":"unknown"},{"key":"C","text":"unorganized"},{"key":"D","text":"undisturbed"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"13.","reason":"before archaeologists would return 决定\"无人扰动\" 线索在时间状语：for decades before archaeologists would return——考古学家此后数十年才回来发掘，期间\"无人发掘扰动\"。同时上文已说Flemming first spotted the sunken __(10)__，遗址已被知晓，不是\"未知\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"unchallenged 多指观点、地位\"未受挑战\"，与遗址的物理状态无关。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"上文已说弗莱明首次发现并制图，遗址已被知晓；若用 unknown\"无人知晓\"，会与上文逻辑直接打架。这是本题最大干扰项。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"unorganized\"杂乱无章\"形容人或活动的组织状态，不形容一处遗址的存在状态。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"undisturbed\"未被打扰/未被扰动\"，正好刻画\"遗址虽已被发现，但数十年无人继续发掘扰动\"，与下文In 2009 archaeologists ... __(14)__ the excavation的\"恢复发掘\"对位。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":14,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"suspended"},{"key":"B","text":"transferred"},{"key":"C","text":"resumed"},{"key":"D","text":"canceled"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"14.","reason":"上段中断 → 本段恢复 线索跨段：the site would lie __(13)__ for decades before archaeologists would return说明几十年中断，In 2009点明时间节点，本空动词必为\"恢复\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"suspend\"中止、暂停\"与本段\"重启发掘\"的剧情完全相反；这本是上一段的状态。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"transfer\"转移\"指换地点或换主体，文中并未提到把发掘工作转给别人或别处。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"resume\"恢复、重启\"与上一段\"中断数十年\"完美对位，且 resume the excavation 是地道搭配。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"cancel\"取消\"会让 2009 年这段重启努力的剧情无从展开，与下文用新技术取得成果直接矛盾。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":15,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"policies"},{"key":"B","text":"theories"},{"key":"C","text":"documents"},{"key":"D","text":"techniques"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"15.","reason":"下句列举的全是技术手段 线索在下句：The team __(16)__ robotics, sonar mapping, and state-of-the-art graphics——机器人、声呐、先进图形技术都属于\"技术 techniques\"，反推上一句中与 tools 并列的应是\"技术\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"policies\"政策\"是行政层面，与机器人、声呐这些技术手段不在同一语义场。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"theories\"理论\"虽然也能取得进步，但与 tools 并列且与下句具体技术对应的，应是\"技术\"而非\"理论\"。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"documents\"文献\"与 tools 不构成自然并列，也与下句具体技术无关。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"techniques 与 tools 同属\"技术手段\"层面，能与下句 robotics、sonar mapping、graphics 这些具体技术形成总—分对应，逻辑严密。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":16,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"ordered"},{"key":"B","text":"provided"},{"key":"C","text":"employed"},{"key":"D","text":"adjusted"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"16.","reason":"to survey the site 表目的 线索是目的状语：to survey the site——使用这些技术的目的是为了勘测遗址，本空动词应表\"使用、运用\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"order\"订购、命令\"与三类技术作宾语不合（不能\"订购声呐测绘\"）。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"provide\"提供\"意味着团队向外提供这些技术，但实际上团队是使用方而非提供方。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"employ\"运用、采用\"是较正式的\"使用\"，搭配 robotics、sonar mapping、graphics 自然，且与 to survey the site 的目的链衔接。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"adjust\"调整\"通常针对设备参数，不用于刚介绍登场的整类技术。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":17,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"effect"},{"key":"B","text":"light"},{"key":"C","text":"reality"},{"key":"D","text":"mind"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"固定搭配","stemZh":"17.","reason":"From 2009 to 2013 揭示成果 线索就在前置时间状语：From 2009 to 2013 they were able to bring the underwater town to __(17)__——四年发掘把水下城\"重见天日\"，与习语 bring to light 完美对应。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"bring ... into effect 常用 into 而非 to，且主语通常是法律法规等抽象政策，与遗址不合。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"bring sth to light 意为\"使……重见天日/被揭示\"，与考古发掘揭示水下城的语境严丝合缝。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"bring sth to reality 不是规范习语；表\"实现\"通常是 bring sth into reality 且用于抽象目标。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"bring sth to mind 意为\"让人想起\"，主语通常是某事物，但本句要表\"使遗址重现\"，与\"使人想起\"不符。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":18,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"crossed"},{"key":"B","text":"connected"},{"key":"C","text":"blocked"},{"key":"D","text":"altered"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"18.","reason":"courtyards 与 50 栋建筑的整体格局 线索是城市格局描述：Covering about two and a half acres, Pavlopetri's three main roads __(18)__ some 50 rectangular buildings, all of which had open courtyards——三条主路串起约50栋带庭院的建筑，描述的是城市规划上的\"连接、贯通\"关系。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"cross\"横穿\"侧重\"穿过\"，常用于穿越一个区域或一条线，但本句要表道路串起诸多建筑的\"连通\"，不是单纯穿越。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"connect 表\"连接\"，三条主路把50栋建筑连为一体，正合城市规划描述的本意。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"block\"阻断\"与道路本职功能相悖，且与下文展示城市繁荣的氛围不符。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"alter\"改变\"无法描述道路与建筑之间的静态格局关系。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":19,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"expecting"},{"key":"B","text":"suggesting"},{"key":"C","text":"predicting"},{"key":"D","text":"recalling"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"语法形态","stemZh":"19.","reason":"loom weights → 纺织业兴旺的推断 线索是逻辑关系：revealed a large number of Minoan-style loom weights是证据，Pavlopetri was a thriving trade center是结论，本空必须表\"由证据指向结论\"。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"expect\"期待\"指向未来，但本句结论是对过去帕夫洛佩特里的状态判断，时间向不对。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"suggest 表\"表明、暗示\"，由发现的大量织布机砝码自然推出\"帕夫洛佩特里是繁荣贸易中心\"的结论，逻辑严密、搭配地道。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"predict\"预测\"也指向未来，且预测对象通常是未发生事件；这里是回顾历史，逻辑不合。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"recall\"回忆\"主语通常是有意识的人，而本句主语隐含为前面的发现/砝码，无法主动\"回忆\"。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}},{"n":20,"stem":"","options":[{"key":"A","text":"robust"},{"key":"B","text":"diverse"},{"key":"C","text":"marginal"},{"key":"D","text":"dependent"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"词义辨析","stemZh":"20.","reason":"thriving trade center 同向呼应 线索就在同句：Pavlopetri was a thriving trade center with a __(20)__ textile industry——\"繁荣的贸易中心 + ___ 的纺织业\"，前后形容词色彩同向，本空必为正向、强势之意。","options":{"A":{"mark":"","note":"robust 表\"强劲、繁盛\"，与 thriving 同向，形容支柱产业稳健有力，是描述产业兴旺的高频选词。"},"B":{"mark":"","note":"diverse 表\"多样\"，强调种类丰富，但本句关注产业强弱而非多样性；与上下文重心不符。"},"C":{"mark":"","note":"marginal\"边缘的、微不足道的\"与 thriving 完全相反；若纺织业边缘化，怎能支撑繁荣贸易？逻辑直接打架。"},"D":{"mark":"","note":"dependent\"依赖他人的\"暗示弱势，且需要带 on，与产业自身实力的描述不合。"}},"choiceEn":"","clues":[]}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-1","kind":"reading","title":"Text 1","textNo":1,"directions":"Directions: Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (40 points)","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 1","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-1","text":"The grammar school boy from Stratford-upon-Avon has landed a scholarly punch after groundbreaking research showed that Shakespeare does benefit children's literacy and emotional development. But only if you act him out."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-2","text":"A study found that a \"rehearsal room\" approach to teaching Shakespeare broadened children's vocabulary and the complexity of their writing as well as their emotional literacy. \"The research shows that the way actors work makes a big difference to the way children use language and also how they think about themselves,\" Jacqui O'Hanlon of the Royal Shakespeare Company (RSC), which commissioned the study, said."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-3","text":"The randomised control trial involved hundreds of year 5 pupils aged nine and ten—at 45 state primary schools that had not been \"previously exposed to RSC pedagogy\". They were split into target and control groups and asked to write, for example, a message in a bottle as Ferdinand after the shipwreck in *The Tempest*. The target group was given a 30-minute drama-based activity to accompany the passage."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-4","text":"The peer-reviewed results showed that the target group of pupils drew on a wider vocabulary, used words \"classed as more sophisticated or rarer\", and wrote at greater length. They also \"appear to be more comfortable writing in role … while [control] pupils imagine how they themselves would react to being shipwrecked, [target] children put themselves in the shoes of a literary character and express that character's emotion\"."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-5","text":"The Time to Act study also found that while control pupils relied on \"desert island clichés\" such as palm trees, target pupils were \"more expansive [giving] a broader picture of the sky, the sea and the atmospheric conditions\"."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-6","text":"O'Hanlon said she had been most surprised by the \"emotional literacy that was evident in the [target] children's writing\" and that they were \"more resilient in their writing, more hopeful\". She added: \"The emotional understanding was very evident and it is probably related to the [rehearsal room process] where you are used to trying to imagine your way through. They were comfortable in describing different emotional states and part of what you do in drama is put yourself in different shoes.\" The study showed the importance of embedding arts in education, she said."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-1-7","text":"But could the results be replicated with any old dramatist? O'Hanlon said more research would be needed but suggested that Shakespeare's use of 20,000 words, compared with the everyday 2,000 words, gave a \"massive expansion of language into children's lives\", which was combined with children \"using their whole bodies to bring words to life\"."}]},"questions":[{"n":21,"stem":"The \"rehearsal room\" approach requires pupils to___________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"rewrite the lines from Shakespeare"},{"key":"B","text":"watch RSC actors' performances"},{"key":"C","text":"play the roles in Shakespeare"},{"key":"D","text":"study drama under RSC artists"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"\"排练室\"教学法要求学生__________。","choicesZh":{"A":"改写莎士比亚的台词","B":"观看RSC演员的表演","C":"扮演莎士比亚剧中的角色","D":"在RSC艺术家指导下学习戏剧"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The \"rehearsal room\" approach requires pupils to___________. 是要求填具体做法的不完整陈述句，没有 infer/suggest/imply 之类推断信号词，属于典型细节题。","parse":"题干 The \"rehearsal room\" approach requires pupils to___________. 是一个待补全的陈述句，主语 The \"rehearsal room\" approach，谓语 requires，宾语 pupils，不定式 to ___ 是要填的部分，结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。","locateEn":"\"The research shows that the way actors work makes a big difference to the way children use language and also how they think about themselves,\" Jacqui O'Hanlon of the Royal Shakespeare Company (RSC), which commissioned the study, said.","locateZh":"皇家莎士比亚剧团（RSC，本研究的委托方）的 Jacqui O'Hanlon 说：\"这项研究表明，演员的工作方式深深改变了孩子们使用语言的方式，也改变了他们对自己的认识。\"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"全文没有任何”重写/改写台词”的描述，rewrite 是凭空加上去的动作；这是利用题干”requires pupils to“求动作的预期，给一个看似合理但文中没有的动作。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文要求孩子\"act him out\"亲身演出，而 watch RSC actors’ performances 把动作从\"演\"偷换成\"看\"——主动变被动，与原文意旨相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判要的就是\"演\"，C 选项 play the roles in Shakespeare 直接对应\"演员工作方式\"与\"act him out\"，是最干净的同义替换。","pairs":[{"orig":"the way actors work","opt":"play the roles in Shakespeare"},{"orig":"if you act him out","opt":"play the roles in Shakespeare"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文 RSC 是研究的委托方，不是教学执行方；孩子是被引导用\"演员方式\"工作，不是\"在 RSC 艺术家手下学戏剧\"。该选项把研究合作关系夸大成”直接师从”。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":22,"stem":"The study divided the pupils into two groups to find whether___________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the change in instruction enhances learning outcomes"},{"key":"B","text":"expanding vocabulary helps develop reading fluency"},{"key":"C","text":"emotion affects understanding of sophisticated works"},{"key":"D","text":"the classroom activity stimulates interest in the arts"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"细节题","stemZh":"该研究将学生分为两组以发现__________。","choicesZh":{"A":"教学方式的改变是否能提升学习成果","B":"扩大词汇量是否有助于培养阅读流畅性","C":"情感是否影响对复杂作品的理解","D":"课堂活动是否能激发对艺术的兴趣"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The study divided the pupils into two groups to find whether___________. 问的是分组实验本身要检验什么，可在原文中找到对应陈述，不需做跨段推断。","parse":"题干 The study divided the pupils into two groups to find whether___________. 中 to find whether 是目的状语，whether 引导宾语从句，从句内容正是要填的部分；理解关键是看清”分组”是手段，”find whether ...“才是目的。","locateEn":"They were split into target and control groups and asked to write, for example, a message in a bottle as Ferdinand after the shipwreck in *The Tempest*. The target group was given a 30-minute drama-based activity to accompany the passage.","locateZh":"他们被分成 target（实验）组和 control（对照）组，被要求以《暴风雨》中船难之后的 Ferdinand 身份写一封”漂流瓶里的信”。target 组额外获得了一个 30 分钟的戏剧化活动来配合该段落。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"唯一变量是教学方式的改变，被观测的产出是写作/学习上的表现，A 选项把”变量→产出”的因果检验完整覆盖，与预判一致。","pairs":[{"orig":"a 30-minute drama-based activity","opt":"the change in instruction"},{"orig":"The peer-reviewed results showed that the target group of pupils drew on a wider vocabulary, used words \"classed as more sophisticated or rarer\", and wrote at greater length.","opt":"enhances learning outcomes"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"扩展词汇是该实验的部分结果之一，不是分组想检验的初衷；而且 reading fluency \"阅读流畅度\"原文从未提及，把写作产出偷换成阅读能力。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文 P6 的因果是\"戏剧化方法→情感素养→更好的写作\"，而 emotion affects understanding of sophisticated works 把方向反过来变成\"情感→对高级作品的理解\"，且 sophisticated works 也偷换了原文 sophisticated words（高级用词）。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"戏剧化活动在本研究中是\"手段\"（独立变量），不是要被检验的\"对艺术的兴趣\"这一结果；arts 取自 P6 末尾的 embedding arts in education 的余韵，被强行嫁接到研究目的上。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":23,"stem":"Control pupils' reliance on \"desert island clichés\" shows their ___________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"weakness in description"},{"key":"B","text":"omission of small details"},{"key":"C","text":"casual style of writing"},{"key":"D","text":"preference for big words"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"推断题","stemZh":"对照组学生对\"荒岛陈词滥调\"的依赖显示出他们__________。","choicesZh":{"A":"在描述方面的薄弱","B":"对细小细节的忽略","C":"随意的写作风格","D":"对大词的偏好"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 Control pupils' reliance on \"desert island clichés\" shows their ___________. 用 shows 把原文现象指向其反映的”特质”，需要从描写一步推到性质判断。","parse":"题干 Control pupils' reliance on \"desert island clichés\" shows their ___________. 主语是动名词性短语 Control pupils' reliance on ... clichés，谓语 shows，宾语 their ___ 待填；理解要点是 reliance on clichés“对陈词滥调的依赖”本身就是负面色彩，正确答案应延续这一负面方向。","locateEn":"The Time to Act study also found that while control pupils relied on \"desert island clichés\" such as palm trees, target pupils were \"more expansive [giving] a broader picture of the sky, the sea and the atmospheric conditions\".","locateZh":"\"是时候行动\"研究还发现：control 组的学生只靠\"沙漠岛俗套\"（比如棕榈树）来写，而 target 组的学生\"更加宏阔，呈现出关于天空、大海与大气状况的更广阔画面\"。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"陈词滥调一词本身就是写作描述贫弱的标准外显，A 选项与预判完全吻合。","pairs":[{"orig":"relied on \"desert island clichés\" such as palm trees","opt":"weakness in description"},{"orig":"target pupils were \"more expansive [giving a broader picture of the sky, the sea and the atmospheric conditions\"]]","opt":"weakness in description"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"control 组不是\"遗漏了小细节\"，而是\"画面单调\"；原文反差是\"狭窄 vs 宏阔\"，不是\"漏 vs 不漏\"；而且 small details 在原文没有任何对应。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"clichés 是明确负面的词，原文是在批评 control 组写作贫弱；casual style of writing 把这种负面评价淡化为\"随意的写作风格\"这一中性甚至略带褒义的描述，性质方向错。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"preference for big words 描述的是\"偏爱大词\"——这正是 target 组的特征（words \"classed as more sophisticated or rarer\"），不是 control 组；而且 palm trees 显然不是 big words。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":24,"stem":"What can promote children's emotional literacy according to O'Hanlon?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Writing in an imaginative manner."},{"key":"B","text":"Identifying with literary characters."},{"key":"C","text":"Drawing inspiration from nature."},{"key":"D","text":"Concentrating on real-life situations."}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据O'Hanlon的观点,什么能促进儿童的情感素养?","choicesZh":{"A":"以富有想象力的方式写作。","B":"与文学人物产生共鸣。","C":"从自然中汲取灵感。","D":"专注于现实生活情境。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 What can promote children's emotional literacy according to O'Hanlon? 限定到 O'Hanlon 的原话，需要在文中找她的引语，属人物观点细节题。","parse":"题干 What can promote children's emotional literacy according to O'Hanlon? 是一个 wh-疑问句，主语是疑问代词 What，谓语 can promote，according to O'Hanlon 是观点来源状语；理解关键是绑定 O'Hanlon 这一信源，不能用全文其他来源的描述代替。","locateEn":"She added: \"The emotional understanding was very evident and it is probably related to the [rehearsal room process] where you are used to trying to imagine your way through. They were comfortable in describing different emotional states and part of what you do in drama is put yourself in different shoes.\"","locateZh":"她补充道：“情感理解非常明显，这很可能与”排练室式过程”有关——在那种过程中，你习惯于一路靠想象去走通。他们能从容地描述各种不同的情绪状态，而戏剧的一部分就是把自己放进不同的鞋里（换位）。”","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"imaginative manner 是写作方式的形容，但 O'Hanlon 给的因不是\"用想象力写\"，而是\"代入别人去想象\"；imaginative manner 只描述了想象这一表层动作，没有抓住”代入他人\"这一关键机制。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文反复强调”穿别人的鞋”+“代入文学角色”，B 选项用 identifying with literary characters 把这两层意思合二为一，与预判精准吻合。","pairs":[{"orig":"put yourself in different shoes","opt":"Identifying with literary characters."},{"orig":"[target children put themselves in the shoes of a literary character and express that character's emotion]]","opt":"Identifying with literary characters."}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Drawing inspiration from nature 取自 P5 a broader picture of the sky, the sea and the atmospheric conditions，那段讨论的是写作描述力，与情感素养的成因无关；O'Hanlon 没把情感素养归因于自然。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"O'Hanlon 强调的是\"代入别人/角色\"，与 Concentrating on real-life situations\"专注于真实生活情境\"方向相反——真实生活情境恰恰是 control 组的反应（[control pupils imagine how they themselves would react to being shipwrecked]]）。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":25,"stem":"It can be inferred from the last paragraph that ___________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the new teaching method may work best with Shakespeare"},{"key":"B","text":"the language of Shakespeare may be formidable for pupils"},{"key":"C","text":"other old dramatists may be included in primary education"},{"key":"D","text":"pupils may be reluctant to work on other old dramatists"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从最后一段可以推断出__________。","choicesZh":{"A":"这种新教学法对莎士比亚作品可能效果最佳","B":"莎士比亚的语言对学生来说可能令人生畏","C":"其他古代剧作家可能被纳入小学教育","D":"学生可能不愿意学习其他古代剧作家的作品"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 It can be inferred from the last paragraph that ___________. 含 inferred，是教科书级别的推断题信号。","parse":"题干 It can be inferred from the last paragraph that ___________. 中 It 是形式主语，真正的主语是 that 引导的填空从句，from the last paragraph 是范围状语；结构清晰，无陷阱。","locateEn":"But could the results be replicated with any old dramatist? O'Hanlon said more research would be needed but suggested that Shakespeare's use of 20,000 words, compared with the everyday 2,000 words, gave a \"massive expansion of language into children's lives\", which was combined with children \"using their whole bodies to bring words to life\".","locateZh":"但同样的结果换成任何一位老剧作家都能复制吗？O'Hanlon 说还需要更多研究，但暗示莎翁所用 2 万词汇——相较于日常的 2 千词汇——给了”语言进入孩子生活的一次巨大扩展\"，再加上孩子\"用整个身体把词带活\"。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"反问句给出\"未必能换其他剧作家\"的暗示，数据对比给出\"莎翁词汇量独一无二\"的证据，二者合起来推出\"该方法在莎翁身上最有效\"，与 A 选项中 may work best 的弱化措辞完全匹配。","pairs":[{"orig":"could the results be replicated with any old dramatist?","opt":"the new teaching method may work best with Shakespeare"},{"orig":"Shakespeare's use of 20,000 words, compared with the everyday 2,000 words, gave a \"massive expansion of language into children's lives\"","opt":"the new teaching method may work best with Shakespeare"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文 gave a \"massive expansion of language into children's lives\" 是正面评价——给孩子带来语言的巨大扩展；formidable for pupils 把这种正面扩展曲解为\"对学生而言令人畏惧\"，色彩相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文反问句 could the results be replicated with any old dramatist? 加 more research would be needed 是 O'Hanlon 对\"换其他剧作家\"的明显保留；other old dramatists may be included in primary education 把这种保留当成肯定推荐，方向反了。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"原文整段从未谈及学生对其他老剧作家是否抵触；reluctance 是凭空增加的学生情绪。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-2","kind":"reading","title":"Text 2","textNo":2,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 2","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-1","text":"I was shocked to learn recently that some scientists want to scale back their research in an effort to decrease carbon emissions. The crisis is here, they said, and we need to cut back on our energy-intensive modelling. At the very least, we need to make our energy use far more sustainable."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-2","text":"It is unarguable that our laboratories, scientific instruments, rockets and satellites—the tools we scientists need to measure the planet's pulse—demand significant amounts of energy both in their construction and operation. And it is equally true that science's unrelenting appetite for information has caused a mushrooming of energy-intensive data centres around the world. According to the International Energy Agency, these buildings now consume about 1 per cent of the world's electricity."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-3","text":"However, this is a price we must pay for understanding the world. How can we inform decision makers about the best ways to bring down carbon emissions if we can't track the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere, where it's coming from and who's producing it? The carbon emissions from technological research are well spent: ultimately this research will safeguard the future of our planet."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-4","text":"It can be hard for scientists to make the case because our work is complex, often takes place behind closed doors and does not always lend itself to easy interpretation or explanation. But demonstrating the efficacy of science will be crucial if we are to solve humanity's greatest challenges."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-5","text":"Recognising the hope that science and engineering can bring was the impetus behind the creation of the Millennium Technology Prize, which is now entering its 20th year as a celebration of human ingenuity. One of the past winners, Professor Martin Green from the University of New South Wales, Australia, is the inventor of the Passivated Emitter and Rear Cell technology which is now found in most of the world's solar panels. Thanks to his invention, we have a real chance to decrease the world's carbon emissions."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-6","text":"Every day, scientists, technologists and engineers are discovering new ways to exploit renewable energy sources and develop techniques not just to use power more intelligently but to power our intelligence. A great example of this is Europe's largest supercomputer, LUMI in Finland, which is astonishingly carbon-negative. Established in an old paper mill, it is powered by a nearby river and its remote heat warms the people who live in the surrounding town of Kajaani."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-2-7","text":"If the world is to meet its net-zero ambitions, we must think hard about how we can deliver sustainable computing and deliver more LUMIs."}]},"questions":[{"n":26,"stem":"The author expressed great surprise at some scientists' ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"unwillingness to cut carbon emissions"},{"key":"B","text":"intention to reduce their research"},{"key":"C","text":"suspicions about sustainable energy"},{"key":"D","text":"waste of electricity in their projects"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者对一些科学家的________表示非常惊讶。","choicesZh":{"A":"不愿削减碳排放","B":"减少研究的意图","C":"对可持续能源的怀疑","D":"在项目中浪费电力"},"analysis":{"type":"The author expressed great surprise at some scientists' ________. 问的是「作者对科学家的什么感到吃惊」，要的是一个具体事实而非推断。","parse":"主干是 The author expressed great surprise at some scientists'，空白处补的是 scientists' 后面的所有格名词，即「科学家的某种做法/想法」；'s 表所属，不是缩写。","locateEn":"I was shocked to learn recently that some scientists want to scale back their research in an effort to decrease carbon emissions.","locateZh":"我最近吃惊地得知，一些科学家想要缩减他们的科研工作，以求降低碳排放。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文是科学家「想减碳→缩减科研」，unwillingness to cut carbon emissions 反过来说他们不愿减碳，与原文意图相反。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"intention to reduce their research 把预判中的「想要」和「缩减科研」整对兑现，与定位句完全等值。","pairs":[{"orig":"scale back their research","opt":"reduce their research"},{"orig":"some scientists want to","opt":"intention to"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"首段只讲他们想 make our energy use far more sustainable，是赞成可持续，没有任何怀疑。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"P2 只承认 significant amounts of energy 的高消耗，但不是浪费；首段更没有「浪费」之说。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":27,"stem":"The author believes that carbon emissions from research ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"have caused grave consequences"},{"key":"B","text":"have aroused groundless worries"},{"key":"C","text":"are hard to handle at present"},{"key":"D","text":"are justifiable in the long run"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"作者认为研究产生的碳排放________。","choicesZh":{"A":"已造成严重后果","B":"已引起毫无根据的担忧","C":"目前难以处理","D":"从长远来看是合理的"},"analysis":{"type":"The author believes that carbon emissions from research ________. 找的是作者直接陈述的观点，原文有明确语句对应，不需要跨段推断。","parse":"句子结构是 The author believes that + 从句；从句主语 carbon emissions from research 后面留空让你补谓语，因此选项给的是动词短语对该主语的评价。","locateEn":"However, this is a price we must pay for understanding the world. … The carbon emissions from technological research are well spent: ultimately this research will safeguard the future of our planet.","locateZh":"然而，这是我们为理解世界必须付出的代价。……科技研究产生的碳排放是值得花的：归根到底，这些研究将守护我们星球的未来。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文只承认科研耗能大，但作者明确为之辩护；从未说它已造成 grave consequences。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"作者只为科研碳排辩护，没说担忧 groundless；他承认耗能事实，并不否认担忧本身。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"作者关心的是长远价值，未提「目前难处理」；at present 是凭空加入的时间限定。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"are justifiable in the long run 同时落实了「值得」和「长远」两个核心要素，与作者评价句一一对应。","pairs":[{"orig":"are well spent","opt":"are justifiable"},{"orig":"ultimately this research will safeguard the future of our planet","opt":"in the long run"}]}}}}},{"n":28,"stem":"The example of Green in Paragraph 5 is used to illustrate ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the achievements of great scientists"},{"key":"B","text":"the urgency of addressing climate change"},{"key":"C","text":"the rewards of scientific endeavours"},{"key":"D","text":"the value of fostering human ingenuity"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"第5段中Green的例子用于说明________。","choicesZh":{"A":"伟大科学家的成就","B":"应对气候变化的紧迫性","C":"科学努力的回报","D":"培养人类创造力的价值"},"analysis":{"type":"The example of Green in Paragraph 5 is used to illustrate ________. 显式使用 example…is used to illustrate 句式，要的是举例的「为何举」。","parse":"主干 The example…is used to illustrate ____；is used to do 是被动表「被用来做某事」，问的是举例的目的；介词短语 in Paragraph 5 修饰 example，限定该例的位置。","locateEn":"Recognising the hope that science and engineering can bring was the impetus behind the creation of the Millennium Technology Prize, which is now entering its 20th year as a celebration of human ingenuity.","locateZh":"正是出于对科学与工程所能带来希望的肯定，才有了千禧科技奖的设立；该奖如今已步入第 20 年，意在表彰人类的智慧创造。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文确实写到 Green 的发明成就，但例证目的不是夸他个人，而是借「他得奖」来体现奖项的存在意义；选 A 等于把例子当成论点。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"P5 末句虽说 Green 的发明给减碳带来 a real chance，但并未渲染气候 urgency；段落中心也不是讨论问题紧迫性。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Green 是「获奖者」，其作用是体现「科研贡献被奖励」；the rewards of scientific endeavours 正是此中心论点的浓缩。","pairs":[{"orig":"was the impetus behind the creation of the Millennium Technology Prize","opt":"the rewards of scientific endeavours"},{"orig":"Recognising the hope that science and engineering can bring","opt":"the rewards of scientific endeavours"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"a celebration of human ingenuity 只是奖项的定语描述；段落真正的中心是「奖励/认可这种智慧带来的希望」，而非「培育智慧」。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":29,"stem":"It can be learned from the last two paragraphs that LUMI ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"is a model of sustainability efforts"},{"key":"B","text":"is a triumph against energy shortage"},{"key":"C","text":"owes much to global net-zero initiatives"},{"key":"D","text":"aims to explore the power of intelligence"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从最后两段可以得知LUMI________。","choicesZh":{"A":"是可持续发展努力的典范","B":"是对抗能源短缺的胜利","C":"在很大程度上归功于全球净零排放倡议","D":"旨在探索智能的力量"},"analysis":{"type":"It can be learned from the last two paragraphs that LUMI ________. 用 can be learned，要求由原文一步推得，不是抄答。","parse":"It can be learned from… that…：It 是形式主语，真正的主语是 that 引导的同位语从句；从句主语 LUMI 后留空，需要补对 LUMI 的整体判断。","locateEn":"A great example of this is Europe's largest supercomputer, LUMI in Finland, which is astonishingly carbon-negative. Established in an old paper mill, it is powered by a nearby river and its remote heat warms the people who live in the surrounding town of Kajaani.","locateZh":"一个绝佳的例子就是欧洲最大的超算——位于芬兰的 LUMI，它令人惊讶地实现了碳负排放。它建在一座旧造纸厂中，由附近的河流供能，其余热则温暖着周边卡亚尼镇的居民。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"第 6 段以 LUMI 为可持续计算的例子，第 7 段呼吁复制更多 LUMI，两段合起来正好说明 LUMI 是 sustainability efforts 的样板。","pairs":[{"orig":"A great example of this","opt":"is a model of sustainability efforts"},{"orig":"we must think hard about how we can deliver sustainable computing and deliver more LUMIs","opt":"is a model of sustainability efforts"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"原文谈的是耗能与碳排，未把 LUMI 描述为应对 energy shortage 的「胜利」；shortage 是原文没有的视角。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"原文是「(造) 更多 LUMI 服务于 net-zero 目标」，并非 LUMI 得益于 net-zero initiatives；选项把因果倒置。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"to power our intelligence 是段首作者形容科技工作的修辞表达，并非 LUMI 的目的；LUMI 的功能是 supercomputer + carbon-negative。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":30,"stem":"Which of the following statements would the author agree with?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Emission-free modelling demands extra funding."},{"key":"B","text":"The need for supercomputers is difficult to meet."},{"key":"C","text":"Energy-intensive research work is inevitable."},{"key":"D","text":"The goals of researchers ought to be realistic."}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"作者会同意下列哪项陈述?","choicesZh":{"A":"零排放建模需要额外资金。","B":"对超级计算机的需求难以满足。","C":"高能耗的研究工作是不可避免的。","D":"研究人员的目标应当现实。"},"analysis":{"type":"Which of the following statements would the author agree with? 不提供任何关键词，要求从作者全文立场出发去筛选选项。","parse":"Which of the following statements would the author agree with？特殊疑问句 + 虚拟语气 would；句末介词 with 的宾语是 which 引导的从句主语，问的是「作者会赞同下列哪句」。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文从未谈到「无排放建模需要额外经费」；emission-free modelling 在原文也无对应，资金问题完全不在作者议题之内。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"P6-P7 是号召造「更多 LUMI」，落点在「能做、要做」，而非感叹「难满足」；这是把作者的积极号召反着说。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Energy-intensive research work is inevitable 同时呼应 P2 的耗能事实、P3 的必要代价立场、P5-P7 的「继续做、做得更可持续」号召，是作者立场的精准浓缩。","pairs":[{"orig":"this is a price we must pay for understanding the world","opt":"Energy-intensive research work is inevitable"},{"orig":"demand significant amounts of energy","opt":"Energy-intensive research work is inevitable"},{"orig":"The carbon emissions from technological research are well spent","opt":"Energy-intensive research work is inevitable"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"作者讨论的是「该不该缩研」「能否可持续」的方法论问题，没有涉及科研目标本身要 realistic 的劝告。","pairs":[]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-3","kind":"reading","title":"Text 3","textNo":3,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 3","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-1","text":"Ever since taking on Netflix Inc. at its own game, old Hollywood has struggled to turn a profit in streaming, with the likes of Disney+, Peacock and Paramount+ losing billions of dollars each year, sparking concerns that the services will never be as profitable as cable once was. But the age of streaming has been a boon for some unintended winners: pirates that use software to rip a film or television show in seconds from legitimate online video platforms and host the titles on their own, illegitimate services, which rake in about $2 billion annually from ads and subscriptions."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-2","text":"With no video production costs, illegal streaming sites have achieved profit margins approaching 90%, according to the Motion Picture Association (MPA), a trade group representing Hollywood studios that's working to crack down on the thousands of illegal platforms that have cropped up in recent years."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-3","text":"Initially the rise of legitimate online businesses such as Netflix actually helped curb digital piracy, which had largely been based on file uploads. But now piracy involving illegal streaming services as well as file-sharing costs the US economy about $30 billion in lost revenue a year and some 250,000 jobs, estimates the US Chamber of Commerce's Global Innovation Policy Center. The global impact is about $71 billion annually."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-4","text":"\"The people who are stealing our movies and our television shows and operating piracy sites are not mom and pop operations,\" says Charlie Rivkin, chief executive officer of the MPA. \"This is organized crime.\" Rivkin joined the MPA in 2017 after the organization failed five years earlier to build consensus between Hollywood and Silicon Valley to win passage of legislation in Congress aimed at stopping online piracy. In 2017 the association formed the Alliance for Creativity and Entertainment (ACE), an enforcement task force of about 100 detectives circling the globe to help local authorities arrest streaming pirates."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-5","text":"ACE says it's helped shrink the number of illegal streaming services in North America to 126, from more than 1,400 in 2018, aided in part by the MPA's support for a 2020 federal law that made large-scale streaming of copyrighted material a serious crime."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-3-6","text":"Consulting firm Parks Associates predicts that legitimate US streaming services' cumulative loss from piracy since 2022 will reach $113 billion in the next two years. \"While there is some optimism that emerging countermeasures and best practices may see piracy begin to plateau by 2027, there is no consensus among stakeholders as to when it may begin to decline,\" says analyst Steve Hawley."}]},"questions":[{"n":31,"stem":"According to Paragraph 1, legitimate streaming services ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"have drawn lessons from Hollywood"},{"key":"B","text":"have surpassed cable in revenue"},{"key":"C","text":"are unpopular with advertisers"},{"key":"D","text":"are confronted with a real threat"}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第1段,合法的流媒体服务________。","choicesZh":{"A":"从好莱坞吸取了教训","B":"在收入上已超过有线电视","C":"不受广告商欢迎","D":"面临真正的威胁"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 According to Paragraph 1 明确指向段内陈述，要求复述事实而非推断。","parse":"legitimate streaming services ________ 是一个待补全的不完整陈述句，主语是 legitimate streaming services，空格处补谓语；四个选项分别提供不同动词短语，比较选项时只需看与主语在第一段事实是否匹配，结构本身无陷阱。","locateEn":"Ever since taking on Netflix Inc. at its own game, old Hollywood has struggled to turn a profit in streaming, with the likes of Disney+, Peacock and Paramount+ losing billions of dollars each year, sparking concerns that the services will never be as profitable as cable once was. But the age of streaming has been a boon for some unintended winners: pirates that use software to rip a film or television show in seconds from legitimate online video platforms and host the titles on their own, illegitimate services, which rake in about $2 billion annually from ads and subscriptions.","locateZh":"自从在 Netflix 的主场上跟它较量起，老牌好莱坞就一直难以在流媒体上盈利，Disney+、Peacock、Paramount+ 等每年亏损数十亿美元，令人担忧这些服务永远赶不上当年有线电视的利润；但流媒体时代倒成全了一些 \"意外赢家\"——盗版者用软件几秒钟就能从正版在线视频平台上扒下一部电影或电视剧、放到自己的非法网站上播放，每年从广告和订阅里捞约 20 亿美元。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"have drawn lessons from Hollywood 把 Hollywood 当 \"被学习的对象\"，但原文 taking on Netflix Inc. at its own game, old Hollywood 明说是 Hollywood 跟 Netflix 学，方向恰好倒置。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"have surpassed cable in revenue 直接说正版流媒体收入已超过有线电视；但原文 sparking concerns that the services will never be as profitable as cable once was 是 \"担心永远赶不上有线电视\"，恰好相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"are unpopular with advertisers 把广告商对正版流媒体的态度凭空塞进文本；第一段只提到非法网站从 ads and subscriptions 捞钱，未谈广告商对正版的好恶。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"预判中的两层 (自家亏损 + 被盗版扒) 直接对应 are confronted with a real threat：a real threat 同时覆盖盈利困境和盗版蚕食，是预判的最简概括。","pairs":[{"orig":"pirates that use software to rip a film or television show in seconds from legitimate online video platforms and host the titles on their own, illegitimate services, which rake in about $2 billion annually from ads and subscriptions","opt":"are confronted with a real threat"},{"orig":"has struggled to turn a profit in streaming","opt":"are confronted with a real threat"}]}}}}},{"n":32,"stem":"It can be learned that streamers like Netflix ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"played a part in the fight against illegal file-sharing"},{"key":"B","text":"reaped benefits from the war with digital pirates"},{"key":"C","text":"promised to become big job creators in the US"},{"key":"D","text":"used to collaborate with file-uploading platforms"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"推断题","stemZh":"可以得知像Netflix这样的流媒体平台________。","choicesZh":{"A":"在打击非法文件共享的斗争中发挥了作用","B":"从与数字盗版的战争中获得了好处","C":"承诺成为美国的重要就业机会创造者","D":"曾与文件上传平台合作"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 It can be learned that streamers like Netflix ________ 使用 It can be learned 引导，要求由原文事实出发做一步合理推论。","parse":"It can be learned that streamers like Netflix ________ 是 It can be learned that 引导宾语从句的不完整结构，主语 streamers like Netflix 在从句内，空格补从句谓语；like 在这里是介词 \"诸如\"，不是动词，基础薄弱考生易误读为 \"喜欢\"，须留意。","locateEn":"Initially the rise of legitimate online businesses such as Netflix actually helped curb digital piracy, which had largely been based on file uploads.","locateZh":"起初，像 Netflix 这样的合法在线生意的崛起，其实帮助遏制了数字盗版，而当时的盗版主要靠文件上传维系。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"原文事实 \"Netflix 帮助压制以文件上传为主的盗版\" 一步推 \"Netflix 在打击非法文件分享中扮演了角色\"，正是 played a part in the fight against illegal file-sharing。","pairs":[{"orig":"Initially the rise of legitimate online businesses such as Netflix actually helped curb digital piracy, which had largely been based on file uploads","opt":"played a part in the fight against illegal file-sharing"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"reaped benefits from the war with digital pirates 是 \"Netflix 从与盗版的对战中获利\"；但原文里获利的是盗版方 (上一段 unintended winners… pirates)，Netflix 们反而 has struggled to turn a profit in streaming，正反角色颠倒。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"promised to become big job creators in the US 谈的是 Netflix 们承诺创造大量岗位；第三段只提 some 250,000 jobs 的损失，且是盗版造成的、不是 Netflix 承诺的就业贡献。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"used to collaborate with file-uploading platforms 说 Netflix 曾与文件上传平台合作；但定位句 helped curb digital piracy, which had largely been based on file uploads 明示 Netflix 是 \"遏制\" 文件上传盗版，而非合作。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":33,"stem":"It can be inferred from Paragraph 4 that the MPA ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"was denied cooperation by Silicon Valley"},{"key":"B","text":"led a national protest against online piracy"},{"key":"C","text":"was urged to form an enforcement task force"},{"key":"D","text":"failed to win support from local authorities"}],"answer":"A","explanation":{"answer":"A","type":"推断题","stemZh":"从第4段可以推断出MPA________。","choicesZh":{"A":"被硅谷拒绝合作","B":"领导了一场反对网络盗版的全国性抗议","C":"被敦促组建执法特别工作组","D":"未能赢得地方当局的支持"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 It can be inferred from Paragraph 4 明示推断属性。","parse":"It can be inferred from Paragraph 4 that the MPA ________ 与 32 题结构同型，主语 the MPA，谓语待补；选项均为被动或主动短语，须分清动作发出者与承受者，否则会和 33 题 A、D 两项的施受方向搞反。","locateEn":"Rivkin joined the MPA in 2017 after the organization failed five years earlier to build consensus between Hollywood and Silicon Valley to win passage of legislation in Congress aimed at stopping online piracy.","locateZh":"Rivkin 于 2017 年加入 MPA，此前五年，MPA 曾试图在好莱坞和硅谷之间凝聚共识、推动国会通过一项反在线盗版的立法，但失败了。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"MPA 推动立法时与硅谷未达共识、立法泡汤，这件事的最经济解读就是硅谷拒绝合作，正对应 was denied cooperation by Silicon Valley。","pairs":[{"orig":"failed five years earlier to build consensus between Hollywood and Silicon Valley to win passage of legislation in Congress aimed at stopping online piracy","opt":"was denied cooperation by Silicon Valley"}]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"led a national protest against online piracy 说 MPA 领导了全国抗议；原文只提推动 legislation in Congress 与成立 Alliance for Creativity and Entertainment (ACE)，与 \"全国抗议\" 无关。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"was urged to form an enforcement task force 说 MPA 是 \"被敦促\" 才组建 ACE；但原文 In 2017 the association formed the Alliance for Creativity and Entertainment (ACE) 是 MPA 自发组建，并无任何 \"被外界催促\" 的描述。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"failed to win support from local authorities 把不合作方说成 local authorities；原文里不合作方是 Silicon Valley，且 about 100 detectives circling the globe to help local authorities 反而显示 ACE 与各地警方是合作关系。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":34,"stem":"According to Hawley, digital piracy ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"cannot be checked in spite of new legislation"},{"key":"B","text":"will possibly overwhelm legitimate streamers"},{"key":"C","text":"is unlikely to diminish in the near future"},{"key":"D","text":"has been underestimated by some analysts"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据Hawley的说法,数字盗版________。","choicesZh":{"A":"尽管有新的立法也无法被遏制","B":"可能会压垮合法的流媒体平台","C":"在不久的将来不太可能减少","D":"被一些分析师低估了"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 According to Hawley, digital piracy ________ 复述特定人物的观点，属事实型细节题。","parse":"According to Hawley, digital piracy ________ 主语 digital piracy，谓语空缺；四个选项均为含情态动词或被动的复合谓语，特别注意 is unlikely to diminish 是 \"不太可能减少\"，双重含蓄否定，考生易误判为肯定。","locateEn":"While there is some optimism that emerging countermeasures and best practices may see piracy begin to plateau by 2027, there is no consensus among stakeholders as to when it may begin to decline.","locateZh":"尽管有人乐观地认为，新的对策与最佳实践或许会让盗版在 2027 年趋于平稳，但各方对于盗版何时开始下降仍无共识。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"cannot be checked in spite of new legislation 加进 \"in spite of new legislation\"，把 a 2020 federal law 拉来作 \"对照失败\" 的对象；但 Hawley 的话只谈走势与共识，并未否定立法的效果，是替 Hawley 替别人答非所问。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"will possibly overwhelm legitimate streamers 把 \"压垮正版流媒体\" 这一结论塞给 Hawley；他只谈走势与共识，并未做出 \"盗版会压垮正版\" 这种更进一步的预测。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Hawley 明说 \"何时开始下降\" 都无共识，连乐观面也只是 2027 年才能趋稳；由此一步推 \"短期内盗版不太可能减少\"，正对 is unlikely to diminish in the near future。","pairs":[{"orig":"there is no consensus among stakeholders as to when it may begin to decline","opt":"is unlikely to diminish in the near future"},{"orig":"emerging countermeasures and best practices may see piracy begin to plateau by 2027","opt":"is unlikely to diminish in the near future"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"has been underestimated by some analysts 说一些分析师低估了盗版；原文里 Hawley 自己就是分析师，并未评价 \"其他分析师有没有低估\"，无任何文本依据。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":35,"stem":"Which of the following is emphasized in the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"The need to coordinate anti-piracy action."},{"key":"B","text":"The criminal nature of copyright violation."},{"key":"C","text":"The prospect of eliminating online piracy."},{"key":"D","text":"The economic harm from illegal streaming."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"文中强调了以下哪一点?","choicesZh":{"A":"协调反盗版行动的必要性。","B":"侵犯版权的犯罪性质。","C":"消除网络盗版的前景。","D":"非法流媒体造成的经济损害。"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 Which of the following is emphasized in the text? 不限定段落、问全文强调，是典型主旨题。","parse":"Which of the following is emphasized in the text? 是疑问句，问 \"以下哪项被本文强调\"；emphasize 是 \"反复强调\"，比 mention 强、比 prove 弱，正确项必须是文中多处回扣的内容，不是只出现一次。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"The need to coordinate anti-piracy action. 把第四段 failed five years earlier to build consensus between Hollywood and Silicon Valley 的局部信息当全文主旨；文章只在第四段顺带提了一次共识失败，其余段落并不围绕 \"协调\" 展开。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"The criminal nature of copyright violation. 只能对应第四段 This is organized crime 与第五段 a serious crime 两处，文章重点不是定罪、而是算账。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"The prospect of eliminating online piracy. 暗示文章在谈消除盗版的前景；但末段 no consensus among stakeholders as to when it may begin to decline 明白说没人知道何时开始下降，全文对 \"消除\" 持谨慎甚至悲观态度。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"全文反复出现的不是 \"如何打击\"、不是 \"是否犯罪\"、也不是 \"能否消灭\"，而是 \"损失了多少钱、多少岗位\"，The economic harm from illegal streaming. 正是这条主线的最简概括。","pairs":[{"orig":"rake in about $2 billion annually from ads and subscriptions","opt":"The economic harm from illegal streaming."},{"orig":"piracy involving illegal streaming services as well as file-sharing costs the US economy about $30 billion in lost revenue a year and some 250,000 jobs","opt":"The economic harm from illegal streaming."},{"orig":"legitimate US streaming services' cumulative loss from piracy since 2022 will reach $113 billion in the next two years","opt":"The economic harm from illegal streaming."}]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-a-4","kind":"reading","title":"Text 4","textNo":4,"directions":"","points":10,"passage":{"title":"Text 4","paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-1","text":"Visit any antiques store and you'll encounter artifacts from the past: photographs, letters, a brochure detailing the Sinclair dinosaur exhibit from the 1964-65 World's Fair, the ephemera of history. Yet these objects aren't truly ephemeral, because they're still here, decades, even centuries later. Why? Because they're tangible."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-2","text":"Have you pondered the life cycle of intangible formats, digital information, given that those who produce these artifacts seldom make provision for their long-term preservation? For millennia, we've known what we've known due to artifacts that have survived, often despite their original creators' neglect. The thing itself is the medium that delivers the information. At the time of creation, no attempts were made at intentional preservation, yet analog materials have a chance of surviving and serving as the historical record that biographers, historians, and novelists rely on. Libraries and archives have traditionally shouldered the responsibility of organization, preservation, and access to information. Librarians digitize the tangible so that researchers the world over can quickly search and access their holdings. The result is an embarrassment of historical riches, which brings its own needle-and-haystack problems."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-3","text":"Librarians' selfless devotion can act against us when users point to universality of access by holding up a cell phone and saying, \"it's all in here\" as evidence that libraries are less vital for researchers today. Yet how was that universality of access made possible and, perhaps more importantly, how is it maintained? Who curates what is preserved? When it comes to born-digital information, the terrifying answer can be: if not librarians and archivists, then no one. Digital information requires a great deal more care than analog."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-4","text":"Even when a digital object is preserved, it may only be the carrier that's saved, not the information itself. As technology advances and a format becomes obsolete, the object is useless. Have you ever stared helplessly at a ZIP disk, thinking: how do I get the files off this? Without constant migration of digital assets, a nightmare about the foreseeable future is what keeps historians up at night: a historical record that abruptly stops when digital replaces analog."},{"id":"p-section2-part-a-4-5","text":"As a librarian whose day job revolves around special collections and digital assets, I share the night terrors of historians, and I'd be lying if I said a comprehensive preservation solution currently exists. Yet researchers can take some comfort in the fact that there are a multitude of librarians devoted to discovering, organizing, and preserving digital information for researchers current and future. While future researchers may find the digital realm a challenging place to ply their trade, they won't find it an impossible one."}]},"questions":[{"n":36,"stem":"The author mentions the artifacts from the past to ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"introduce the collection of antiques"},{"key":"B","text":"contrast them with everyday items"},{"key":"C","text":"bring up the issue of preservation"},{"key":"D","text":"comment on their historical value"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"作者提到过去的文物是为了________。","choicesZh":{"A":"介绍古董的收藏","B":"将它们与日常物品进行对比","C":"提出保存的问题","D":"评论它们的历史价值"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 The author mentions the artifacts from the past to 用「mention X to do」结构，问的是举例服务于哪一个论点。","parse":"题干 The author mentions the artifacts from the past to 是一个待补全句，主干为 The author mentions ... to + 选项动词原形，to 引出目的状语，无陷阱。","locateEn":"Yet these objects aren't truly ephemeral, because they're still here, decades, even centuries later. Why? Because they're tangible.","locateZh":"然而这些东西并非真正转瞬即逝，因为几十年甚至几百年后它们依然在这里。为什么？因为它们是有形的。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"introduce the collection of antiques 把作者引子里 antiques store 当成主题；可作者讲古董店只是借场景举例，全文真正主题是保存，不是收藏。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"contrast them with everyday items 提到的「与日常物对比」原文从未出现，第一段只把 artifacts 视为留存样本，没有任何 everyday items 出现。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"预判「引出保存议题」与 bring up the issue of preservation 在动词（引出 vs bring up）与宾语（议题 vs the issue of preservation）上一一对应，直接锁定 C。","pairs":[{"orig":"Yet these objects aren't truly ephemeral, because they're still here, decades, even centuries later. Why? Because they're tangible.","opt":"bring up the issue of preservation"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"comment on their historical value 谈历史价值；原文 Yet these objects aren't truly ephemeral 讨论的是「为何能留存」，不是「它们有多少历史价值」。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":37,"stem":"Compared with digital objects, tangible artifacts ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"are less subject to their creators' neglect"},{"key":"B","text":"convey information in a more direct way"},{"key":"C","text":"require more intentional preservation"},{"key":"D","text":"are less likely to suffer serious damage"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"与数字对象相比,有形文物________。","choicesZh":{"A":"较少受到其创造者的忽视","B":"以更直接的方式传达信息","C":"需要更有意识的保存","D":"不太可能遭受严重损坏"},"analysis":{"type":"题干 Compared with digital objects, tangible artifacts 不含 infer/imply 等推断信号，问的是作者直接陈述的对比属性。","parse":"题干 Compared with digital objects, tangible artifacts 中 Compared with digital objects 是过去分词短语作状语，逻辑主语是 tangible artifacts，主干仍是 tangible artifacts + 选项谓语。","locateEn":"The thing itself is the medium that delivers the information.","locateZh":"物件本身就是承载并传递信息的媒介。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"are less subject to their creators' neglect 称有形物较少被创作者忽视；原文 often despite their original creators' neglect 恰恰说有形物常在创作者疏忽下仍然留存，并未说创作者对它们更上心。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"其它三项谈的是 neglect/preservation/damage，全在「保存难度」维度，与定位句「传递机制」不在同一语义场，故 B 是唯一与预判匹配的。","pairs":[{"orig":"The thing itself is the medium that delivers the information.","opt":"convey information in a more direct way"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"require more intentional preservation 称有形物需要更刻意的保存；原文 At the time of creation, no attempts were made at intentional preservation 恰说创作时没有刻意保存的努力，与选项相反。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"are less likely to suffer serious damage 说有形物不易受损；原文未在「受损程度」上比较有形与数字，第二段谈的是创作者保存意图与留存机会，不涉及损伤难度。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":38,"stem":"According to Paragraph 3, librarians' work may result in ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"oversupply of materials"},{"key":"B","text":"undervaluation of libraries"},{"key":"C","text":"researchers' underperformance"},{"key":"D","text":"users' overreliance on technology"}],"answer":"B","explanation":{"answer":"B","type":"细节题","stemZh":"根据第3段,图书馆员的工作可能导致________。","choicesZh":{"A":"材料供应过剩","B":"图书馆价值被低估","C":"研究人员表现不佳","D":"用户过度依赖技术"},"analysis":{"type":"librarians' work may result in 问的是行为带来的具体结果，是段内客观信息的直接转述。","parse":"题干 According to Paragraph 3, librarians' work may result in 主干 work may result in + 选项名词性短语；情态动词 may 仅是缓和语气，不是推断信号。","locateEn":"Librarians' selfless devotion can act against us when users point to universality of access by holding up a cell phone and saying, \"it's all in here\" as evidence that libraries are less vital for researchers today.","locateZh":"图书馆员的无私付出反过来会害了我们——用户举起手机说「一切都在这里」，以此为据声称图书馆对今日的研究者已没那么重要。","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"oversupply of materials 指材料供过于求；原文 embarrassment of historical riches, which brings its own needle-and-haystack problems 谈的是「检索难」（大海捞针），而非数量上的供过于求。且该句在第二段，不属于 Q38 限定的 Paragraph 3。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"预判「让用户贬低图书馆的价值」与 undervaluation of libraries 在主体（libraries）和动作（贬低/低估）上完全对应，因此锁 B。","pairs":[{"orig":"libraries are less vital for researchers today","opt":"undervaluation of libraries"}]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"researchers' underperformance 称研究者表现不佳；第三段只说用户「认为图书馆不重要」，未评价研究者本身的表现高低。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"users' overreliance on technology 把矛头对准「用户过度依赖科技」；原文谈的是用户因此低估图书馆的价值，而不是评价用户依赖科技本身是好是坏。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":39,"stem":"The \"ZIP disk\" is cited as an example to show ________.","options":[{"key":"A","text":"the hazard of retrieving files through unusual means"},{"key":"B","text":"the infeasibility of constantly migrating digital assets"},{"key":"C","text":"the possibility of losing information in obsolete formats"},{"key":"D","text":"the inconvenience of storing information on analog devices"}],"answer":"C","explanation":{"answer":"C","type":"例证目的题","stemZh":"\"ZIP磁盘\"被引用作为例子来说明________。","choicesZh":{"A":"通过非常规方式检索文件的危险","B":"持续迁移数字资产的不可行性","C":"在过时格式中丢失信息的可能性","D":"在模拟设备上存储信息的不便"},"analysis":{"type":"The \"ZIP disk\" is cited as an example to show 直接点明本题问举例的目的。","parse":"题干 The \"ZIP disk\" is cited as an example to show 中被动语态 is cited 的施事是作者，to show 引出目的状语，主语 ZIP disk 加引号强调原词出现。","locateEn":"As technology advances and a format becomes obsolete, the object is useless. Have you ever stared helplessly at a ZIP disk, thinking: how do I get the files off this?","locateZh":"随着技术演进、格式过时，存储物件便变得毫无用处。你是否曾无助地盯着一张 ZIP 盘心想：要怎么把里面的文件取出来？","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"the hazard of retrieving files through unusual means 暗示作者警告「用非常手段取文件存在危险」；原文只问 how do I get the files off this，没有提及 unusual means 或 hazard 这类延伸。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"the infeasibility of constantly migrating digital assets 说「持续迁移数字资产不可行」；原文 Without constant migration of digital assets 把持续迁移视为解决办法而非「不可行」，逻辑相反。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"C 项 obsolete formats 与原文 a format becomes obsolete 直接对应，losing information 又能解释「文件取不出」的具体场景，是预判到答案的直接落点。","pairs":[{"orig":"As technology advances and a format becomes obsolete, the object is useless.","opt":"the possibility of losing information in obsolete formats"},{"orig":"Have you ever stared helplessly at a ZIP disk, thinking: how do I get the files off this?","opt":"the possibility of losing information in obsolete formats"}]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"the inconvenience of storing information on analog devices 把不便归到 analog devices；ZIP disk 是数字载体（digital），原文也专谈数字物的脆弱，张冠李戴。","pairs":[]}}}}},{"n":40,"stem":"Which of the following statements best summarizes the text?","options":[{"key":"A","text":"Hard work should be done to preserve artifacts."},{"key":"B","text":"Contributions of librarians should be recognized."},{"key":"C","text":"Accessing databases is essential to researchers."},{"key":"D","text":"Keeping digital historical records is a challenge."}],"answer":"D","explanation":{"answer":"D","type":"主旨题","stemZh":"以下哪项陈述最能概括本文?","choicesZh":{"A":"应该努力保存文物。","B":"图书馆员的贡献应该得到认可。","C":"访问数据库对研究人员至关重要。","D":"保存数字历史记录是一项挑战。"},"analysis":{"type":"Which of the following statements best summarizes the text 直接问全文主旨。","parse":"题干 Which of the following statements best summarizes the text 是疑问句，主语 Which of the following statements，谓语 best summarizes，宾语 the text，结构清晰，无陷阱。","locateEn":"","locateZh":"","options":{"A":{"flag":"","note":"Hard work should be done to preserve artifacts. 用泛泛的 artifacts 代替「数字记录」，没有点出数字时代这个核心矛盾；且文章主线不是泛泛号召 hard work，而是揭示数字保存的难处与图书馆员角色。","pairs":[]},"B":{"flag":"","note":"Contributions of librarians should be recognized. 只截取第三段「图书馆员被低估」一面，没覆盖第四段 ZIP disk 例子与第五段表态；文章主旨是数字记录保存的挑战，图书馆员的贡献只是论证中的一环。","pairs":[]},"C":{"flag":"","note":"Accessing databases is essential to researchers. 谈数据库访问的必要性；原文 universality of access 只是作为「用户低估图书馆」的引子，文章并未论证数据库访问对研究者的必要性。","pairs":[]},"D":{"flag":"","note":"D 项把全文「难」+ 「可努力」的态度合在「a challenge」一个词里，覆盖 P2–P5 全部内容，且与作者既忧又持希望的口吻一致。","pairs":[{"orig":"a nightmare about the foreseeable future is what keeps historians up at night: a historical record that abruptly stops when digital replaces analog","opt":"Keeping digital historical records is a challenge."},{"orig":"I'd be lying if I said a comprehensive preservation solution currently exists","opt":"Keeping digital historical records is a challenge."}]}}}}}]},{"id":"section2-part-b","kind":"ordering","title":"Section II Part B 段落排序","directions":"Directions: The following paragraphs are given in a wrong order. For questions 41-45, you are required to reorganize these paragraphs into a coherent text by choosing from the list A-H and filling them into the numbered boxes. Paragraphs A, C and H have been correctly placed. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET . (10 points).","points":10,"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-b-1","label":"A","text":"Peters likes to photograph butterflies in a landscape, celebrating the beauty of their surroundings as well as the insects themselves. His pictures of a Glanville fritillary rising from the sea-pinks beside the chalk cliffs of Compton Bay on the Isle of Wight are particularly glorious. These take-off shots are even more challenging because they require a wide-angle lens, which means he must be less than 2cm from the butterfly. It's incredibly difficult to get that close to a skittish, sun-warmed insect. Unlike some photographers, who \"cheat\" by keeping insects in a fridge to slow them down, Peters refuses to tamper with wild butterflies."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-2","label":"B","text":"Peters' signature shot is a butterfly \"take-off\", showing the multiple wing-beats of one butterfly in one frame as it lifts off from a flower. How does he capture it? Technology helps. A typical digital SLR camera shoots 20 frames a second. He uses a high-speed OM System which shoots 120 frames a second."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-3","label":"C","text":"Britain has relatively few butterfly species compared with mainland Europe and 80% are in decline, mostly because intensive chemical farming has reduced many species to tiny fragments of habitat and small nature reserves. Global heating is benefiting some species but others are too isolated to find suitable new habitat, and gardening habits—paving over gardens and using pesticides—aren't helping either. Butterflies may not pollinate as many plants as wild bees and hoverflies, but because British butterflies are the best-studied group of insects in the world, they are an extremely useful indicator of the wider declines in flying insects."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-4","label":"D","text":"Five years ago, at summer's end, Andrew Fusek Peters was diagnosed with bowel cancer. \"I was waiting for surgery, feeling really ill, sitting in my garden. It was amazing weather and there were painted lady butterflies everywhere,\" he says. \"They were a symbol of fragile life, of hope and defiance, and something appealed to my soul.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-5","label":"E","text":"That makes it sound easy, and artificial, but Peters insists it is still a massive challenge. He typically takes between 10,000 and 20,000 shots to get one butterfly take-off sequence in focus. At such high shutter speeds, the depth of field is tiny, and as butterflies do not fly in a straight line they swiftly flutter out of focus. As well as thousands of attempts, it takes patience and fieldcraft to anticipate a butterfly's likely flight-line and catch it—in focus."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-6","label":"F","text":"So what's the appeal of a long, sweaty day in pursuit of an elusive, fast-moving wild animal? \"It just feels bloody brilliant,\" says Peters. \"If I've had a full day of good encounters with butterflies, met interesting butterfly people and I've got some good shots, that becomes a vault in my spiritual bank. It's a happy feeling.\""},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-7","label":"G","text":"A children's author and poet who had become a keen amateur photographer, Peters watched the butterflies and idly wondered if he could capture them in flight. It swiftly became an obsession as he recovered from a successful operation to remove the cancer. In recent summers, he has travelled the length and breadth of Britain to photograph all 58 native species of butterfly. Now the fruits of those summers have been published in a beautiful new book."},{"id":"p-section2-part-b-8","label":"H","text":"A butterfly takes off so quickly it is still impossible to react quickly enough to capture that take-off but if he half-presses the shutter, the camera saves the 70 previous frames before the moment he actually takes the picture. \"It's time travel, so I don't miss the moment of take-off,\" he says. After he's captured the butterfly taking off, he layers 10 to 15 frames together in Photoshop."}],"choices":[{"key":"A","text":"Peters likes to photograph butterflies in a landscape, celebrating the beauty of their surroundings as well as the insects themselves. His pictures of a Glanville fritillary rising from the sea-pinks beside the chalk cliffs of Compton Bay on the Isle of Wight are particularly glorious. These take-off shots are even more challenging because they require a wide-angle lens, which means he must be less than 2cm from the butterfly. It's incredibly difficult to get that close to a skittish, sun-warmed insect. Unlike some photographers, who \"cheat\" by keeping insects in a fridge to slow them down, Peters refuses to tamper with wild butterflies."},{"key":"B","text":"Peters' signature shot is a butterfly \"take-off\", showing the multiple wing-beats of one butterfly in one frame as it lifts off from a flower. How does he capture it? Technology helps. A typical digital SLR camera shoots 20 frames a second. He uses a high-speed OM System which shoots 120 frames a second."},{"key":"C","text":"Britain has relatively few butterfly species compared with mainland Europe and 80% are in decline, mostly because intensive chemical farming has reduced many species to tiny fragments of habitat and small nature reserves. Global heating is benefiting some species but others are too isolated to find suitable new habitat, and gardening habits—paving over gardens and using pesticides—aren't helping either. Butterflies may not pollinate as many plants as wild bees and hoverflies, but because British butterflies are the best-studied group of insects in the world, they are an extremely useful indicator of the wider declines in flying insects."},{"key":"D","text":"Five years ago, at summer's end, Andrew Fusek Peters was diagnosed with bowel cancer. \"I was waiting for surgery, feeling really ill, sitting in my garden. It was amazing weather and there were painted lady butterflies everywhere,\" he says. \"They were a symbol of fragile life, of hope and defiance, and something appealed to my soul.\""},{"key":"E","text":"That makes it sound easy, and artificial, but Peters insists it is still a massive challenge. He typically takes between 10,000 and 20,000 shots to get one butterfly take-off sequence in focus. At such high shutter speeds, the depth of field is tiny, and as butterflies do not fly in a straight line they swiftly flutter out of focus. As well as thousands of attempts, it takes patience and fieldcraft to anticipate a butterfly's likely flight-line and catch it—in focus."},{"key":"F","text":"So what's the appeal of a long, sweaty day in pursuit of an elusive, fast-moving wild animal? \"It just feels bloody brilliant,\" says Peters. \"If I've had a full day of good encounters with butterflies, met interesting butterfly people and I've got some good shots, that becomes a vault in my spiritual bank. It's a happy feeling.\""},{"key":"G","text":"A children's author and poet who had become a keen amateur photographer, Peters watched the butterflies and idly wondered if he could capture them in flight. It swiftly became an obsession as he recovered from a successful operation to remove the cancer. In recent summers, he has travelled the length and breadth of Britain to photograph all 58 native species of butterfly. Now the fruits of those summers have been published in a beautiful new book."},{"key":"H","text":"A butterfly takes off so quickly it is still impossible to react quickly enough to capture that take-off but if he half-presses the shutter, the camera saves the 70 previous frames before the moment he actually takes the picture. \"It's time travel, so I don't miss the moment of take-off,\" he says. After he's captured the butterfly taking off, he layers 10 to 15 frames together in Photoshop."}],"questions":[{"n":41,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"D"},{"n":42,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"G"},{"n":43,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"B"},{"n":44,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"E"},{"n":45,"stem":"","options":[],"answer":"F"}],"order":["D","G","C","B","H","E","A","F"],"fixed":{"2":"C","4":"H","6":"A"},"chain":"41. → 42. → C → 43. → H → 44. → A → 45.","orderChain":"正确顺序 41 D → 42 G → 预给 C → 43 B → 预给 H → 44 E → 预给 A → 45 F","seams":["【引出后一段】 段 G 指代衔接：G 段首 the butterflies 定冠词回指 D 花园里那群 painted lady 蝴蝶 。D 首次引入主角并写他坐在花园里 there were painted lady butterflies everywhere；G 段首 Peters watched the butterflies 用定冠词特指 the butterflies，指的正是 D 里那群蝴蝶。D 是首段（全名首次登场），其后被 G 的回指接上，故 D 在 41、G 在 42。","【承接前一段】 段 D 指代衔接：G 的 the butterflies 回指 D，且 G 把 D 的「触动」延展为「好奇能否拍下」 。G 段首 Peters watched the butterflies 的定冠词回指 D 花园那群蝴蝶，情节上由 D 的 something appealed to my soul 自然推进到 wondered if he could capture them in flight，是「被打动→起念去拍」的顺承，G 紧接 D 落在 42。","【引出后一段】 段 C 语义承接：G 末尾提到走遍 Britain 拍蝴蝶，C 顺势插叙 Britain 蝴蝶的整体背景 。G 段尾说他 travelled the length and breadth of Britain 拍遍 58 种；given 段 C 紧接着以 Britain has relatively few butterfly species 切入英国蝴蝶的宏观背景，词面 Britain 复现 + 由个人转宏观的插叙，G→C 承接成立（无显式连接词，属较弱的语义承接，作印证）。","【引出后一段】 段 H 词汇复现：B 抛出 take-off 并设问怎么拍，H 复现 take-off 并正面作答 。B 提出招牌 Peters' signature shot is a butterfly \"take-off\" 并自问 How does he capture it?，只答了相机帧率高一半；given 段 H 段首复现 capture that take-off 并补上「半按快门回存前 70 帧」这后半答案。词汇复现 + 问答呼应，B 紧贴在 given 段 H 之前的 43 位。","【承接前一段】 段 H 指代衔接：E 段首的 That 指代 H 段刚讲完的整套「回存帧+Photoshop叠合」技术 。E 开头 That makes it sound easy, and artificial 的 That 必须有所指；H 段刚把相机自动保存 70 帧、再 layers 10 to 15 frames together in Photoshop 这套流程讲完，正是「听着简单又有点假（artificial）」的那件事。指代消解唯一落到 H，故 E 紧接 H 在 44 位。","【引出后一段】 段 A 语义承接：E 收在「对焦极难、需野外功夫」，A 以「在风景中拍起飞更难」递进承接 。E 段尾落在拍清楚之难 catch it—in focus，后接的 given 段 A 用 These take-off shots are even more challenging 顺势把难度再推一层（要广角、贴到 2cm），逻辑上是「难→更难」的递进，承接成立（无显式连接词，属语义承接，弱锚，仅作印证）。","【承接前一段】 段 A 逻辑连接词：F 段首 So 收束 A 等段铺陈的「这么难、还坚持不作弊」，转入「图什么」的总结发问 。given 段 A 把难度推到顶并强调 Peters refuses to tamper with wild butterflies（宁难不作弊）；F 段首用 So 引出 So what's the appeal of a long, sweaty day，正是对前面层层之难的收束式追问。So 的因果收束 + wild 一词复现，F 紧接 given 段 A 落在末位 45。"]},{"id":"section2-part-c","kind":"translation","title":"Section II Part C 英译汉","directions":"Directions: Read the following text carefully and then translate the underlined segments into Chinese. Write your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. ( 10 points)","points":10,"passage":{"paragraphs":[{"id":"p-section2-part-c-1","text":"Innovation and research have relied on public participation in science for centuries. It was a musician who discovered the planet Uranus in the 18th century by making his own telescope with mirrors composed of copper and tin. (46) Recent decades have seen science move into a convention where engagement in the subject can only be done through institutions such as a university. Citizen science provides an opportunity for greater public engagement and the democratisation of science."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-2","text":"In the information era, large data sets, small teams and financial restrictions have slowed scientific process. (47) But by utilising the natural curiosity of the general public it is possible to overcome many of these challenges by engaging non-scientists directly in the research process. Anyone can be a citizen scientist, regardless of age, nationality or academic experience. You don't even need any formal training, just an inquisitive mind and the enthusiasm to join one of the thousands of citizen science projects to generate new knowledge and the means to understand a genuine scientific outcome."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-3","text":"(48) Scientists have employed a variety of ways to engage the general public in their research, such as making data analysis into an online game or sample collection into a smartphone application. They've implored citizen scientists to help with bug counting and categorising cancer cells, and even identifying distant galaxies."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-4","text":"This form of accessible science means that great minds are able to join the race to create and develop projects with the potential to change the world. A citizen science-based approach can extend the field of vision and include more ideas and different brains to problem-solve and create, making innovation faster and more effective."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-5","text":"The rise of citizen science has grown alongside the rise of do-it-yourself biology laboratories around the world. (49) These groups of people are part of a rapidly expanding biotechnological social movement of citizen scientists and professional scientists seeking to take discovery out of institutions and put it into the hands of anyone with the enthusiasm."},{"id":"p-section2-part-c-6","text":"There are around 40 official do-it-yourself biology centres across the globe in locations including Paris, London, Sydney, and Tel Aviv. (50) They pool resources, collaborate, think outside the box, and find solutions and ways around obstacles to explore science for the sake of science without the traditional boundaries of working inside a formal setting. So is it time to take the Petri dish out of the laboratory and into the garage?"}]},"sentences":[{"n":46,"en":"Recent decades have seen science move into a convention where engagement in the subject can only be done through institutions such as a university.","refZh":"近几十年来，科学已逐渐走入一种惯例：对科学领域的参与（或：从事科学研究）只能通过大学等（正式）机构来进行。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Recent decades have seen science move into a convention","lit":"近几十年看到科学走进了一种惯例","role":"（主干（see sb. do 结构，主语为时间））","note":"难点： 时间作主语的 see 结构＝「在……期间，……发生了」 Recent decades have seen science move... 是考研英语一高频句式：用时间段作主语＋see＋宾语＋宾补（动词原形），意思不是「几十年看见了」，而是「近几十年来，科学（发生了）……」。务必把英文的拟人主语「拆掉」、还原为汉语的时间状语。convention 这里也不是「大会」，而是熟词僻义「惯例／常规做法」。"},{"en":"where engagement in the subject can only be done","lit":"在这种惯例中对该学科的参与只能被进行","role":"（where 引导的定语从句（修饰 convention））","note":"难点： where = in which，被动态硬译会绕口 where 不指地点，而是 in which＝「在这一惯例下」。engagement in the subject 是抽象名词短语，subject 指 science（上文话题），可具体化为「对科学的参与／从事科学研究」。can only be done 被动直译「只能被做」很别扭，汉化时转主动或译成「只能通过……进行／开展」。"},{"en":"through institutions such as a university.","lit":"通过像大学这样的机构","role":"（方式状语（through 短语））","note":"难点： such as a university 单数泛指 a university 是单数泛指一类，译成「大学等机构」即可，无需译「一所大学」。institutions 这里偏中性「（科研）机构」，结合上下文「公民科学打破院墙」的基调，可译「（大学这样的）正式机构」。"}]},{"n":47,"en":"But by utilising the natural curiosity of the general public it is possible to overcome many of these challenges by engaging non-scientists directly in the research process.","refZh":"但是，通过利用普通公众与生俱来的好奇心，让非科学家直接参与到研究过程中，就有可能克服许多这样的（科研）难题。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"But by utilising the natural curiosity of the general public","lit":"但是，通过利用普通公众的天然好奇心","role":"（方式状语（by + 动名词短语））","note":"难点： by + V-ing 是方式状语，不是被动 by utilising 不是被动语态，而是「通过……」的方式状语。general public 是固定搭配「普通公众／社会大众」，不要逐字译成「一般的公众」。natural curiosity 译「天然的好奇心／与生俱来的好奇心」皆可，结合本文褒义基调取偏褒义的表达。"},{"en":"it is possible to overcome many of these challenges","lit":"有可能克服许多这些挑战","role":"（主干（it 形式主语＋to do 真主语））","note":"难点： these challenges 必须回上句还原 these challenges 不能孤立译成「这些挑战」，按通读建境锁定，它指 large data sets, small teams and financial restrictions have slowed scientific process，即「庞大数据、小团队、经费紧张」等科研瓶颈。翻译时不必把指代说破，但可灵活处理为「这些（科研）难题／瓶颈」，让读者知道指的是上文那些。it is possible 是常用的形式主语句，对应汉语「就有可能／就能够」。"},{"en":"by engaging non-scientists directly in the research process.","lit":"通过让非科学家直接参与到研究过程中","role":"（方式状语（第二个 by + 动名词短语））","note":"难点： engage sb. in sth. = 让某人参与某事 engage 在本文是高频词（engagement、engaging、engage the general public），统一取「使……参与」的义项，不要译成「雇佣」或「订婚」。non-scientists 是「非科学家／非专业人士」，对应上句的 general public。directly 修饰「参与」的方式，要紧贴动词译「直接参与」。"}]},{"n":48,"en":"Scientists have employed a variety of ways to engage the general public in their research, such as making data analysis into an online game or sample collection into a smartphone application.","refZh":"科学家已经采用了多种方式来让公众参与到他们的研究中，例如把数据分析做成在线游戏，或者把样本采集变成一款手机应用程序。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"Scientists have employed a variety of ways","lit":"科学家已经采用了多种方式","role":"（主干（主谓宾））","note":"难点： employ 在科研语境里＝「采用／使用」 employ 是熟词僻义高发词：日常义「雇佣」，学术英语里高频取「采用／使用／运用」。本句宾语是 ways（方式），与「雇佣」搭不上，必须取「采用」。a variety of ways＝「多种方式／各种各样的方法」，是固定搭配。"},{"en":"to engage the general public in their research,","lit":"来让公众参与到他们的研究中","role":"（目的状语（to do））","note":"难点： engage sb. in sth. 与上句术语一致 再次出现 engage sb. in sth.，与句 47 的 engaging non-scientists in... 句式相同，必须译成「让／使……参与到……」，与全文术语「参与」保持一致。their 指代上文 Scientists 的研究，可译「自己的研究」或省略所有格，按汉语习惯通常省略。"},{"en":"such as making data analysis into an online game","lit":"例如把数据分析做成在线游戏","role":"（举例状语（such as + 动名词））","note":"难点： make A into B = 把 A 变成 B make A into B 是固定结构「把 A 改造成／变成 B」，不要逐字译「使 A 进入 B」。data analysis 是术语「数据分析」，online game 是「在线游戏／网络游戏」。这一块和下一块是 such as 后并列的两个例子，要保持平行结构。"},{"en":"or sample collection into a smartphone application.","lit":"或者把样本采集做成手机应用","role":"（举例状语并列项（省略 making））","note":"难点： 并列省略 making，要补回来 这一块前面省略了重复的 making：or (making) sample collection into a smartphone application。翻译时必须把动词补回，否则汉语会断气。sample collection＝「样本采集／标本收集」，smartphone application＝「手机应用／智能手机程序／手机 App」，三种译法都可接受。"}]},{"n":49,"en":"These groups of people are part of a rapidly expanding biotechnological social movement of citizen scientists and professional scientists seeking to take discovery out of institutions and put it into the hands of anyone with the enthusiasm.","refZh":"这些群体是一场正在迅速壮大的生物技术社会运动的一部分；参与其中的公民科学家和职业科学家力求把科学发现带出（大学等正式）机构，将其交到任何有热情的人手中。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"These groups of people are part of a rapidly expanding biotechnological social movement","lit":"这些群体是一场快速壮大的生物技术社会运动的一部分","role":"（主干（主系表））","note":"难点： These groups of people 指代＋多重定语连缀 These groups of people 按通读建境锁定，指上句 do-it-yourself biology laboratories around the world 里的人，即「这些（DIY 生物实验室的）群体」。表语 a rapidly expanding biotechnological social movement 是「形容词＋形容词＋名词」三层修饰：rapidly expanding（迅速扩大的）→ biotechnological（生物技术的）→ social movement（社会运动），汉语按「迅速扩大的生物技术社会运动」从后往前串即可。"},{"en":"of citizen scientists and professional scientists","lit":"由公民科学家和职业科学家组成的","role":"（后置定语（of 短语，修饰 movement））","note":"难点： of 表「由……组成」 of 在此不是「的」那么单薄，而是表「组成关系」，可译「由……组成」或「公民科学家与职业科学家的」。citizen scientists 与上文术语统一译「公民科学家」；professional scientists 译「专业科学家／职业科学家」，二者并列体现公民科学的「业余＋专业」混合属性。"},{"en":"seeking to take discovery out of institutions","lit":"（这些科学家）力图把发现移出机构","role":"（现在分词后置定语（修饰 scientists））","note":"难点： seeking 的逻辑主语是前面两类 scientists seeking 是现在分词作后置定语，逻辑主语是前面的 citizen scientists and professional scientists，不是 movement。take discovery out of institutions 是「take A out of B」结构，意为「把 A 从 B 中拿出来」；discovery 此处是抽象名词，指「（科学）发现／科研活动」，可具体化为「让科学发现走出机构／让科研走出机构」。institutions 与句 46 的 institutions 呼应，统一译「机构」。"},{"en":"and put it into the hands of anyone with the enthusiasm.","lit":"并把它交到任何有热情的人手中","role":"（并列动作（与 take 并列，分词短语第二项））","note":"难点： it 回指 discovery，put into the hands of＝交给 put it 中的 it 指代前面的 discovery（科学发现／科研活动），翻译时可重复名词「把它（科学发现）交到……手中」或直接译「将科研交给……」。put sth. into the hands of sb. 是固定搭配「把某物交到某人手中」。anyone with the enthusiasm 译「任何有热情的人／凡是有热情的人」，with the enthusiasm 中的 the 暗示「有这份热情」的人，体现作者对公民科学参与者的褒奖。"}]},{"n":50,"en":"They pool resources, collaborate, think outside the box, and find solutions and ways around obstacles to explore science for the sake of science without the traditional boundaries of working inside a formal setting.","refZh":"他们汇集资源、相互协作、跳出常规思维框框，并想方设法寻找解决方案、绕开各种障碍，去探索科学、为科学本身而探索——不再受制于「必须在正规机构内做研究」这一传统束缚。","analysis":"","chunks":[{"en":"They pool resources, collaborate, think outside the box,","lit":"他们汇集资源、协作、跳出常规思考","role":"（主干（主语＋三个并列谓语））","note":"难点： pool 是动词、think outside the box 是习语 三个并列谓语逐个看：① pool 在此是动词「汇集／集中（资源）」，不是名词「水池」；② collaborate＝「合作／协作」；③ think outside the box 是英语习语「跳出框框思考／打破常规思考／发挥创造力」，不能直译「在盒子外面思考」。三个动词节奏明快，汉译时保持短句并列即可。They 按通读建境锁定指上句 around 40 official do-it-yourself biology centres 里的人，译「他们」即可。"},{"en":"and find solutions and ways around obstacles","lit":"并找到解决方案和绕过障碍的办法","role":"（并列谓语第四项）","note":"难点： ways around obstacles＝绕开障碍的办法 around 在此不是「周围」，而是「绕过」的方位义：work around / get around / a way around 是固定搭配「绕开／规避（障碍／困难）」。find solutions and ways around obstacles 可合并译「寻找解决方案、想方设法绕开障碍」或「找到办法克服障碍」，避免重复啰嗦。"},{"en":"to explore science for the sake of science","lit":"为了科学本身去探索科学","role":"（目的状语（to do））","note":"难点： for the sake of science＝为科学而科学 for the sake of sth. 是固定搭配「为了……的缘故」。explore science for the sake of science 是英文里很有韵味的同语反复结构，强调「不为名利／不为应用」的纯粹动机，对应汉语「为科学而科学／纯粹为了科学本身去探索」。这是本句最有「态度色彩」的一笔，体现作者对 DIY 科学家的赞赏。"},{"en":"without the traditional boundaries of working inside a formal setting.","lit":"没有在正式环境中工作的传统边界","role":"（伴随／方式状语（without 短语））","note":"难点： without ＝不受……束缚，formal setting＝正规场所 without 在此是「不受……约束／没有……的限制」，不要硬译「没有」。boundaries 译「边界／束缚／限制」，traditional boundaries 译「传统束缚」更顺。formal setting＝「正规场所／正式环境／（大学等）正规机构」，与全文 institutions 形成呼应，本质都指「体制内的科研环境」。working inside a formal setting 译「在正规机构内开展（科研）工作」。"}]}]},{"id":"section3-part-a","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part A 写作","directions":"51. Directions: Read the following email from your classmate Paul and write him a reply. --- Dear Li Ming, I was really excited to hear that you'd invite some young craftsmen to demonstrate their innovative craft-making on campus. May I know more about what they'll show? Also, I'd like to help with your preparation work. Please let me know what I can do. Yours, Paul --- Write your answer in about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in your email; use\"Li Ming\" instead.(10 points)","prompt":"51. Directions: Read the following email from your classmate Paul and write him a reply. --- Dear Li Ming, I was really excited to hear that you'd invite some young craftsmen to demonstrate their innovative craft-making on campus. May I know more about what they'll show? Also, I'd like to help with your preparation work. Please let me know what I can do. Yours, Paul --- Write your answer in about 100 words on the ANSWER SHEET. Do not use your own name in your email; use\"Li Ming\" instead.(10 points)","points":10,"image":null,"model":{"essay":"Dear Paul, Thank you for your kind message and your warm interest in our craft demonstration. You asked what the young craftsmen will show. They will present innovative works such as paper-cutting reshaped into modern greeting cards, and pottery decorated with bold digital-style patterns, blending old traditions with fresh creative ideas. As for your help, I would be truly grateful if you could design a few eye-catching posters to promote the event, and greet our guests at the entrance on that day. Your talent and energy would make everything run more smoothly. Looking forward to working together with you. Yours, Li Ming","translation":"亲爱的保罗： 感谢你的来信，也谢谢你对我们这次手工艺展示活动如此热情关注。\n你问那些年轻匠人会展示些什么。他们会带来颇具创新的作品，比如把剪纸重新设计成现代风格的贺卡，以及绘上大胆数码风纹样的陶艺，让古老传统与新鲜创意相融合。\n至于你想帮忙的事，如果你能设计几张吸引眼球的宣传海报，并在当天在入口处迎接来宾，我将不胜感激。你的才华和活力一定会让一切进行得更加顺利。\n期待与你并肩筹备。 此致 李明","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"呼应来信开场落地","text":"【第 1 段】 呼应来信开场落地 。开头 \"Thank you for your kind message and your warm interest\" 先接住 Paul 的来信、回应他的热情，正是 paragraphGuide 第 1 段'呼应来信开场'的落点，一句话就把'回信'的身份立住。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"具体示例撑起展示","text":"【第 2 段】 具体示例撑起展示 。用 \"such as paper-cutting reshaped into modern greeting cards, and pottery decorated with bold digital-style patterns\" 抛出两个可想象的具体例子，兑现'具体示例答展示'，末句 \"blending old traditions with fresh creative ideas\" 点明创新，答准了 Paul 的问题。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"礼貌请托派任务","text":"【第 3 段】 礼貌请托派任务 。\"I would be truly grateful if you could design a few eye-catching posters... and greet our guests at the entrance\" 用请托句架派了两项明确任务，落实'派明确任务答帮忙'，并以 \"Your talent and energy would make everything run more smoothly\" 肯定其作用。"},{"tag":"【第 4 段】","core":"收尾落款规范","text":"【第 4 段】 收尾落款规范 。\"Looking forward to working together with you\" 礼貌收尾，紧接 \"Yours, Li Ming\" 规范落款、未用真名，达成'礼貌收尾规范落款'与署名硬约束。"}],"wordCount":102}},{"id":"section3-part-b","kind":"writing","title":"Section III Part B 写作","directions":"52. Directions: Write an essay based on the table below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the table briefly, 2) interpret the table, and 3) give your comments. Write your answer in 160-200 words on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points)","prompt":"52. Directions: Write an essay based on the table below. In your essay, you should 1) describe the table briefly, 2) interpret the table, and 3) give your comments. Write your answer in 160-200 words on the ANSWER SHEET. (20 points)","points":20,"image":{"src":"assets/writing/2025-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg","alt":"这是一张统计表格，其具体构成和数据如下： 图表标题（位于图片正上方）：近年来全国居民平均每百户年末主要耐用消费品拥有量","width":"624","height":"363","local":"assets/writing/2025-english-one-section3-part-b.jpg"},"model":{"essay":"The table above clearly illustrates the changes in the average ownership of major durable consumer goods per 100 households across China from 2014 to 2023. As is shown, the ownership of air conditioners surged dramatically from 75.2 units to 145.9 units, nearly doubling over the decade, while washing machines and refrigerators rose only modestly, from 83.7 to 98.2 and from 85.5 to 103.4 respectively. Several factors may account for this striking contrast. On the one hand, the rapid climb in air conditioners reflects Chinese residents' growing pursuit of a comfortable and higher-quality life as incomes steadily increase. On the other hand, washing machines and refrigerators, as long-standing household necessities, had already approached saturation, leaving limited room for further growth. From my perspective, this trend is a vivid reflection of the remarkable improvement in people's living standards. To sustain such progress, the government and enterprises should continue to boost residents' incomes and produce more energy-efficient appliances, so that a better life becomes accessible to every family.","translation":"上表清晰地展示了 2014 至 2023 年间全国居民平均每百户主要耐用消费品拥有量的变化。如图所示，空调的拥有量从 75.2 台猛增到 145.9 台，十年间近乎翻倍；而洗衣机和电冰箱只是小幅上升，分别从 83.7 升至 98.2、从 85.5 升至 103.4。\n造成这一鲜明对比的原因可能有几个。一方面，空调拥有量的快速攀升反映出随着收入稳步增长，中国居民对舒适、高品质生活的日益追求。另一方面，洗衣机和电冰箱作为长期的家庭刚需，早已接近普及饱和，进一步增长的空间有限。\n在我看来，这一趋势正是人民生活水平显著提高的生动写照。为延续这种进步，政府与企业应继续提高居民收入、生产更多节能家电，让更美好的生活惠及每一个家庭。","breakdown":[{"tag":"【第 1 段】","core":"描述段：主题句＋一组对比","text":"【第 1 段】 描述段：主题句＋一组对比 。首句 The table above clearly illustrates… 用‘总起点题’句架交代主题与 2014-2023 时间跨度；随后只挑一组对比：空调 surged dramatically from 75.2 units to 145.9 units, nearly doubling，另用 while 把洗衣机、电冰箱的小幅上升合并带过，做到了 describe briefly 而非逐格罗列，落实了 no.1 的‘抓极值找对比’。"},{"tag":"【第 2 段】","core":"解读段：一分为二归因","text":"【第 2 段】 解读段：一分为二归因 。Several factors may account for… 引出归因，再用 On the one hand / On the other hand 分别把空调猛涨归因为 growing pursuit of a comfortable and higher-quality life，把另外两类趋缓归因为 approached saturation。数字被上升到收入与需求层面，正是 interpret 要的‘数据背后说明了什么’。"},{"tag":"【第 3 段】","core":"评论段：表态＋建议收束","text":"【第 3 段】 评论段：表态＋建议收束 。From my perspective… 亮出评价——生活水平显著提高的写照；再用 To sustain such progress, … should… 给出提高收入、生产节能家电的建议，兑现分值最高的 comments，并让建议与上段解读自然衔接、闭合全文。"}],"wordCount":165}}],"questionCount":45,"objectiveCount":45,"answerTable":{"ans-section1":"完形填空（四选一） Section I （1–20 题） 1 B prone · 2 C gradually · 3 B submerged · 4 C remains · 5 B off · 6 A currents · 7 D protected · 8 A gathering · 9 A when · 10 D structures · 11 D examine · 12 A Despite · 13 D undisturbed · 14 C resumed · 15 D techniques · 16 C employed · 17 B light · 18 B connected · 19 B suggesting · 20 A robust \n（逐题解析见 完形填空（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-1":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (1) （21–25 题） 21 C · 22 A · 23 A · 24 B · 25 A \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-2":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (2) （26–30 题） 26 B · 27 D · 28 C · 29 A · 30 C \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-3":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (3) （31–35 题） 31 D · 32 A · 33 A · 34 C · 35 D \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-a-4":"阅读理解（四选一） Section II Part A (4) （36–40 题） 36 C · 37 B · 38 B · 39 C · 40 D \n（逐题解析见 阅读理解（四选一）解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-b":"段落排序 Section II Part B 41=D → 42=G → 预给C → 43=B → 预给H → 44=E → 预给A → 45=F \n（逐题解析见 段落排序解析页\n）","ans-section2-part-c":"英译汉（划线句子翻译） Section II Part C \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 英译汉（划线句子翻译）解析页","ans-section3-part-a":"写作 Section III Part A \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页","ans-section3-part-b":"写作 Section III Part B \n主观题 · 参考范文与逐题解析见\n 写作解析页"},"coverage":{"objective":45,"explained":40,"missingSections":[]}};
